WEBVTT

00:00:00.610 --> 00:00:14.890
<v Ira Glass>This reporter named Eric Mennel told me this thing a little while back that I found totally fascinating, knew nothing about at all. Eric was raised as an evangelical in Florida and works these days at Gimlet Media. And he's been reporting on this one part of the Christian world for a while.
<v Ira Glass>一位名叫埃里克·门内尔（Eric Mennel）的记者前阵子告诉我一件我完全着迷、且此前闻所未闻的事情。埃里克在佛罗里达州的一个福音派家庭长大，目前在 Gimlet Media 工作。一段时间以来，他一直在报道基督教世界里的一个特定领域。

00:00:14.890 --> 00:00:31.060
<v Eric Mennel>There's this whole Christian version of Silicon Valley, only instead of, like, creating apps and tech products, what the Christian world is trying to do is use the tools of Silicon Valley to create churches. They'll call them church startups or church plants.
<v Eric Mennel>基督教世界里存在一个硅谷式的版本，只是他们不是在开发应用程序或科技产品，而是试图利用硅谷的手段来创建教会。他们称之为“教会创业”或“教会植堂”。

00:00:31.060 --> 00:00:34.350
<v Ira Glass>And so what are some of the tools that they use that are the same?
<v Ira Glass>那么，他们使用的哪些工具与硅谷相同呢？

00:00:34.350 --> 00:00:50.450
<v Eric  Mennel>It kind of runs the gamut, right? If you can find it in Silicon Valley, it probably exists somewhere in the church planting world. So for example, there are boot camps, where, for maybe three or five days of your time, you can go and learn everything that you need to learn in order to start a new church.
<v Eric  Mennel>种类繁多，应有尽有，对吧？如果你能在硅谷找到的东西，它很可能也存在于教会植堂的世界里。举个例子，有那种训练营，你只要花三到五天的时间，就能学到创立新教会所需的一切知识。

00:00:50.450 --> 00:00:51.010
<v Ira Glass>Mhm.
<v Ira Glass>嗯。

00:00:51.010 --> 00:01:10.430
<v Eric Mennel>They have these big incubators, right? Like, there's one in New York City, where, over the course of two years, you'll come to get this product off the ground-- this product, of course, being your church. And the same way that tech companies are obsessed with their origin stories-- one thing you hear a lot is how they've gotten their start in garages-- church plants have their own origin stories.
<v Eric Mennel>他们有大型孵化器，对吧？比如纽约市就有一个，你可以在两年内让这个“产品”问世——当然，这个产品就是你的教会。正如科技公司热衷于讲述他们的起源故事一样——你经常听到他们说自己是在车库里起家的——教会植堂也有他们自己的起源故事。

00:01:10.430 --> 00:01:17.610
<v Man 1>Over two decades ago, my wife, Amy, and I started Life Church in what was then a little two-car garage.
<v Man 1>二十多年前，我和妻子艾米（Amy）在当时一个只能停两辆车的车库里创办了“生命教会”（Life Church）。

00:01:17.610 --> 00:01:22.180
<v Eric Mennel>That's from a video off one of these churches' websites. They also have conferences.
<v Eric Mennel>那段视频来自这些教会之一的网站。他们也举办会议。

00:01:22.180 --> 00:01:28.670
<v Man 2>We're going to go ahead and get started. Glad you're here. We'll give you one minute. Kind of our background--
<v Man 2>我们马上要开始了。很高兴你们来到这里。我们给你们一分钟时间。谈谈我们的背景——

00:01:28.670 --> 00:01:38.260
<v Eric Mennel>So this is a session at the biggest church planting conference in the country. It happens every year in Orlando. It's called Exponential. It's sort of the TechCrunch Disrupt of church planting.
<v Eric Mennel>这就是全国最大的教会植堂会议上的一场会议。它每年在奥兰多举行，名为“指数级”（Exponential）。它有点像是教会植堂界的 TechCrunch Disrupt 大会。

00:01:38.260 --> 00:01:52.450
<v Man 2>So we ease-- again, our goal, our target, is unchurched people, high-conversion growth rate. So we're targeting them. So we're easing them in to the process of church. Now, there's two gathering models, if you will.
<v Man 2>所以，我们——再次强调，我们的目标是未去过教会的人群，实现高转化率的增长。所以我们要针对他们。我们要引导他们慢慢进入教会的过程。现在，如果可以的话，这里有两种聚会模式。

00:01:52.450 --> 00:01:59.220
<v Ira Glass>So this sounds so jargony-- like, high-conversion growth rate means they're trying to convert non-church people into church people?
<v Ira Glass>这听起来太像行话了——比如说，高转化率是指他们试图把不去教会的人变成去教会的人吗？

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:17.030
<v Eric Mennel>Yeah. It's really interesting. You actually kind of see this, like, blending of techy jargon with Christian jargon in this world. Like, in this particular seminar, they talk about "kingdom return on investment." Or "evangelistic networking" is one I've read, or "corporate renewal dynamics."
<v Eric Mennel>是的。这非常有趣。在这个领域，你实际上可以看到科技术语和基督教术语的融合。比如，在这个特定的研讨会上，他们谈论“王国投资回报率”。我还读到过“福音派网络化”或“企业更新动力学”。

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:25.990
<v Eric Mennel>"Launch" is a big word that they use in both worlds. They talk about "launch Sundays" and "launch budgets" in church planting. And the framing of what they're doing is in business terms, right?
<v Eric Mennel>“启动”（Launch）是他们在两个领域都常用的一个大词。他们谈论教会植堂中的“启动周日”和“启动预算”。他们所做的事情的框架都是用商业术语来表述的，对吧？

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.190
<v Tim Keller>This is a story about--
<v Tim Keller>这是一个关于——

00:02:27.190 --> 00:02:34.910
<v Eric Mennel>This is from a website from one of the gurus of this movement. His name is Tim Keller. I've actually heard one pastor call him the Yoda of church planting.
<v Eric Mennel>这来自该运动的一位大师的网站。他的名字叫蒂姆·凯勒（Tim Keller）。我实际上听过一位牧师称他为教会植堂界的尤达（Yoda）。

00:02:34.910 --> 00:02:35.480
<v Ira Glass>Uh-huh.
<v Ira Glass>嗯哼。

00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:52.670
<v Eric Mennel>He founded this incredibly successful church in New York City called Redeemer Presbyterian church. And it's also formed its own church planting organization called Redeemer City to City. So this is a video about planting more churches in New York City, and it's described entirely through business terms.
<v Eric Mennel>他在纽约市创办了一家非常成功的教会，叫救赎主长老会（Redeemer Presbyterian church）。它还成立了自己的教会植堂组织，叫“救赎主城市到城市”（Redeemer City to City）。这是一段关于在纽约市建立更多教会的视频，它完全是通过商业术语来描述的。

00:02:52.670 --> 00:03:04.460
<v Tim Keller>In 1989, New York was the least religious city in America. Less than 1% of center-city New Yorkers attended a gospel teaching church. Today, that number is 5%.
<v Tim Keller>1989年，纽约是美国最不信教的城市。不到1%的纽约市中心居民参加福音派教会。今天，这个数字是5%。

00:03:04.460 --> 00:03:09.170
<v Eric Mennel>These are their own numbers, by the way. And in the video, a dot is moving up the graph.
<v Eric Mennel>顺便说一下，这些是他们自己的数据。视频里，一个点正在图表上向上移动。

00:03:09.170 --> 00:03:26.900
<v Tim Keller>We are launching a 10-year vision to see the body of Christ in New York rise from 5% to 15%. That means tripling by 2026. And here's the thing. We think that just might represent a tipping point-- a tipping point of gospel saturation that does more than just--
<v Tim Keller>我们正在发起一个十年的愿景，旨在看到纽约的基督身体从5%增长到15%。这意味着到2026年翻三倍。关键在于，我们认为这可能代表一个引爆点——一个福音饱和的引爆点，其作用不仅是——

00:03:26.900 --> 00:03:33.510
<v Ira Glass>OK, so in the tech world, the investors are these VC firms, Venture Capital firms. What's the equivalent in this world?
<v Ira Glass>好吧，在科技界，投资者是那些风险投资公司。那么在这个领域，对应的角色是什么？

00:03:33.510 --> 00:03:53.830
<v Eric Mennel>So a lot of the startup capital comes from the biggest denominations. The Southern Baptists-- they spend tens of millions of dollars a year on church planting. But a lot of church plants actually get their funding directly from megachurches-- established churches that have thousands of members. They view it as their mission to launch new, successful churches.
<v Eric Mennel>很多启动资金来自最大的教派。美南浸信会每年在教会植堂上花费数千万美元。但许多教会植堂实际上直接从大型教会——拥有数千名成员的成熟教会——获得资助。他们将启动新的、成功的教会视为自己的使命。

00:03:53.830 --> 00:03:55.560
<v Ira Glass>And how long has this been happening?
<v Ira Glass>这种情况持续多久了？

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:25.930
<v Eric Mennel>Church planting, in its modern form, started about 20 or 30 years ago. And they came about to solve what is basically a business problem. Since the 1970s, the number of people who've said they are going to church at least once a week has dropped 40%, according to the best data on this. That's from the National Opinion Research Center at the University of Chicago. And it's been especially bad among young people in their late teens and 20s. The percentage of 20-somethings who claim no religious affiliation has doubled since 1996.
<v Eric Mennel>现代形式的教会植堂始于大约20或30年前。它们的出现是为了解决一个本质上的商业问题。据芝加哥大学国家民意调查中心提供的最佳数据显示，自1970年代以来，每周至少去一次教会的人数下降了40%。在十几岁到二十几岁的年轻人中情况尤为严重。自1996年以来，声称没有宗教信仰的20多岁人群的比例翻了一番。

00:04:25.930 --> 00:04:26.680
<v Ira Glass>Wow.
<v Ira Glass>哇。

00:04:26.680 --> 00:04:37.490
<v Eric Mennel>Yeah, from 20% to almost 40%. And these churches have recognized that without young people, they simply cannot survive. So they wanted to get nonbelievers coming to church.
<v Eric Mennel>是的，从20%增加到近40%。这些教会意识到，如果没有年轻人，他们根本无法生存。所以他们想吸引不信教的人来教会。

00:04:37.490 --> 00:04:47.230
<v Eric Mennel>And to do that, what they've tried to do is disrupt the way evangelical church is usually done. So they'll meet in weird, lean, hipper spaces. They'll have better music.
<v Eric Mennel>为了做到这一点，他们试图打破传统的福音派教会运作方式。所以他们会在奇怪、简朴、更潮的空间里聚会。他们会提供更好的音乐。

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.040
<v Ira Glass>And has that worked?
<v Ira Glass>那成功了吗？

00:04:50.040 --> 00:05:01.720
<v Eric Mennel>Yeah, it's worked. When you look at church attendance, one of the only groups that aren't seeing drastically falling numbers are evangelicals-- the group that has focused most seriously on church planting.
<v Eric Mennel>是的，成功了。当你观察教会出席率时，唯一没有看到数字急剧下降的群体之一就是福音派——这个最认真专注于教会植堂的群体。

00:05:01.720 --> 00:05:14.770
<v Ira  Glass>And Eric, you are here today to tell this story. You have spent months following somebody who's trying to attract funders and start one of these church plant startup churches.
<v Ira  Glass>埃里克，你今天来这里是为了讲述这个故事。你花了好几个月的时间跟踪一个正试图吸引资助者并建立其中一家植堂创业教会的人。

00:05:14.770 --> 00:05:30.430
<v Eric Mennel>Yes, yes. His name is Watson Jones III. Watson is 34. He's bald. He has a clean beard and thick-rimmed glasses. He grew up going to a traditional Baptist Church on the south side of Chicago. And he knew that he wanted to start a church, even since he was a teenager.
<v Eric Mennel>是的，是的。他的名字叫沃森·琼斯三世（Watson Jones III）。沃森今年34岁。他光头，留着干净的胡子，戴着粗框眼镜。他在芝加哥南区的一家传统浸信会教会长大。甚至从十几岁起，他就知道自己想创办一家教会。

00:05:30.430 --> 00:05:41.330
<v Eric Mennel>So he did all the things you're supposed to do to become a pastor. He went to seminary. He got a master's in divinity. And from the beginning, he had a real gift for preaching. This is from a sermon he gave while he was still in training.
<v Eric Mennel>所以他做了所有成为牧师该做的事。他去了神学院。他获得了道学硕士学位。从一开始，他在讲道方面就非常有天赋。这是他在接受培训时所做的一次布道。

00:05:41.330 --> 00:05:52.450
<v Watson Jones>Remember growing up on the south side of Chicago, and my daddy was teaching me how to ride a bike. Your parents ever walked alongside of you while you riding that bike? And I remember, we were going down the street, and I saw some girls on the porch.
<v Watson Jones>记得小时候在芝加哥南区，我爸爸教我骑自行车。你们的父母有没有在你骑车时走在你身边？我记得，我们沿着街道走，我看到门廊上有一些女孩。

00:05:52.450 --> 00:06:00.260
<v Watson Jones>Oh, yeah. You know, when you see them girls on the porch, you got to get it together. Can't have your parent holding your hand now. You got to straighten up and fly right.
<v Watson Jones>哦，是的。你知道，当你看到门廊上的那些女孩时，你得表现得像样点。现在不能让你爸牵着你的手了。你得挺直腰板，表现出色。

00:06:00.260 --> 00:06:03.920
<v Watson Jones>I said, Daddy, get your hands off the handlebars, man. I got this.
<v Watson Jones>我说，爸爸，把你的手从车把上拿开，伙计。我可以搞定。

00:06:03.920 --> 00:06:04.840
<v Watson Jones>[AUDIENCE LAUGHING]
<v Watson Jones>[观众笑声]

00:06:04.840 --> 00:06:20.200
<v Watson Jones>And it didn't take me too long to get to a point where I realized, I never learned how to balance myself without my daddy's help. Here it is-- some of us are busy slapping Jesus' hands off the handlebars of our life. There are many of us--
<v Watson Jones>没过多久，我就意识到，如果没有我爸爸的帮助，我根本学不会如何保持平衡。就是这样——我们中有些人正忙着把耶稣的手从我们生活的车把上拍掉。我们中有许多人——

00:06:20.200 --> 00:06:34.220
<v Eric Mennel>And Watson really knew nothing about the church planting world until a friend clued him into some of this, told him about a boot camp that Watson then attended. But Watson didn't understand it was this entire movement-- not until he went to a conference. This was in 2013.
<v Eric Mennel>沃森在朋友向他透露之前，对教会植堂世界一无所知，他告诉了他一个沃森后来参加的训练营。但沃森直到参加了一次会议才明白这是一个完整的运动。那是在2013年。

00:06:34.220 --> 00:06:39.600
<v Eric Mennel>He went to that big conference in Orlando, Exponential. It was eye opening. It was really exciting to him.
<v Eric Mennel>他去了奥兰多的那场大型会议，“指数级”。那让他大开眼界。他感到非常兴奋。

00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:50.360
<v Watson Jones>Some of what I was seeing was so new to me. It was so different. They were wearing skinny jeans and they looked real cool. And I felt like, in my background, we still wearing suits.
<v Watson Jones>我所看到的一些东西对我来说太新了。太不一样了。他们穿着紧身牛仔裤，看起来很酷。我觉得，在我的背景里，我们还是穿着西装。

00:06:50.360 --> 00:07:13.230
<v Watson Jones>And I felt like people in those conferences were talking about things that helped relate with people who were not used to going to church. I felt like they were on the cutting edge. I believed that the only people who were effective were church planters. And the rest of these churches, that they need to close, or they need to adapt to all of that.
<v Watson Jones>我觉得那些会议里的人谈论的事情有助于与那些不习惯去教会的人建立联系。我觉得他们走在时代前沿。我相信只有那些教会植堂者才是有效的。剩下的那些教会，要么需要关闭，要么需要适应所有这些变化。

00:07:13.230 --> 00:07:23.290
<v Eric   Mennel>He did notice something else when he was there at the conference, something that was also very similar to Silicon Valley.
<v Eric   Mennel>他在会议上确实注意到了另一件事，这也是与硅谷非常相似的一点。

00:07:23.290 --> 00:07:34.720
<v Watson Jones>There were at least 4,000 in the room. And I saw a lot of white people. You know, I'd be the only black in the room, because it wasn't really a black thing more than it was a white evangelical thing.
<v Watson Jones>房间里至少有4000人。我看到很多白人。你知道，我是房间里唯一的黑人，因为这与其说是黑人的事情，不如说是白人福音派的事情。

00:07:34.720 --> 00:07:46.440
<v Eric Mennel>That's true in general for this movement. In the United States, it's mostly white evangelicals doing this. And they're mostly planting churches in the suburbs, or affluent or gentrifying areas in cities.
<v Eric Mennel>对于这项运动来说，这通常是事实。在美国，主要是白人福音派在做这件事。他们大多在郊区，或者城市中富裕或正在士绅化的地区建立教会。

00:07:46.440 --> 00:08:04.060
<v Eric Mennel>So Watson wanted to do things the way these white church planters were doing them. But he wanted to fill the church with people like him-- people of color who lived in the inner city. And that difference was, to use tech world language, his competitive advantage. That was the thing that set him apart from thousands of other evangelicals looking for investors.
<v Eric Mennel>所以沃森想用这些白人植堂者的方式做事。但他想让自己的教会里充满像他一样的人——住在市中心、有色人种。用科技界的语言来说，这种差异是他的竞争优势。这就是他从成千上万寻找投资者的福音派中脱颖而出的原因。

00:08:04.060 --> 00:08:22.420
<v Watson  Jones>I saw myself practically dancing between two lines-- between the white church and the black church. I danced in the black church because it was my identity, but danced in the evangelical world because that's where a lot of financial partners were.
<v Watson  Jones>我看到自己在白人教会和黑人教会之间的两条线上跳舞。我在黑人教会里跳舞，因为那是我的身份，但在福音派世界里跳舞，因为那里有许多财务合作伙伴。

00:08:22.420 --> 00:08:37.930
<v Ira Glass>And so, as you're about to hear, Watson jumps into it, like a proper pastoral entrepreneur, to build his church. And some of what that involves is not so different from starting any business, of course. And some of it is very particular to bringing people to God.
<v Ira Glass>正如你即将听到的，沃森像一个正经的牧师创业者一样跳了进去，建立他的教会。当然，这其中涉及的一些事情与创办任何企业并无不同。其中一些是非常特别的，是为了引领人们归向神。

00:08:37.930 --> 00:09:00.140
<v Ira Glass>It's a funny mix where you are taking charge of things, right? You're marching to this future like you can make a difference, while all along, there's this weird powerlessness to it. Like, at some point, no matter what you do, no matter how hard you work, how hard you think about it, people either show up or they don't show up. Either they love your thing or they don't love your thing.
<v Ira Glass>这是一个有趣的组合，你负责掌控事情，对吧？你向着未来进军，仿佛你能有所作为，但同时，这种过程中又有一种奇怪的无力感。就像在某个时刻，无论你做什么，无论你多么努力，无论你多么用心地思考，人们要么出现，要么不出现。要么他们喜欢你的东西，要么不喜欢。

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:10.370
<v Ira Glass>It's like that saying from Field of Dreams-- if you build it, they will come. The real-life version of that usually has a question mark at the end of it. If you build it, will they come?
<v Ira Glass>就像《梦幻之地》里的那句名言——只要你盖好了，他们就会来。现实生活中的版本通常会在后面加个问号。如果你盖好了，他们真的会来吗？

00:09:10.370 --> 00:09:21.600
<v Ira Glass>Today on our show, we have stories about people building stuff and wondering exactly that. From WBEZ Chicago, it's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. And we're just going to jump right in with Act One...
<v Ira Glass>今天在我们的节目中，我们有关于人们建立事物并怀疑这一点的故事。来自芝加哥 WBEZ 电台，这是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯（Ira Glass）。我们直接进入第一幕……

00:09:21.600 --> 00:09:32.560
<v Ira Glass>Act One, Come All Ye Faithless. So Eric Mennel, in this act, explains what happened when Watson started his years-long quest to build his church and all the things that he could only learn the hard way.
<v Ira Glass>第一幕，来吧，所有的不信者。所以埃里克·门内尔在这一幕中，解释了沃森开始他那漫长的教会建设之旅时发生了什么，以及他只能通过艰难困苦才能学到的东西。

00:09:32.560 --> 00:09:46.880
<v Eric Mennel>Part of what's impressive about the church planting world is how thoroughly it tries to prepare its pastors for what's ahead. When he was first getting started, Watson enrolled in this sort of boot camp called Fellowship Associates. They flew him to Arkansas every two weeks for a few days to train.
<v Eric Mennel>教会植堂界令人印象深刻的部分在于它非常彻底地试图为牧师们的前路做准备。刚开始时，沃森报名参加了一个叫“团契同工”（Fellowship Associates）的训练营。他们每两周让他飞往阿肯色州几天进行培训。

00:09:46.880 --> 00:10:02.680
<v Watson Jones>They teach you everything, from fundraising, to developing a core team, to writing a philosophy of ministry, which is basically a business prospectus, to crafting a budget, to funding the budget, to managing donors, to gathering people around a vision and an idea.
<v Watson Jones>他们教你一切，从筹款到开发核心团队，到编写事工哲学（基本上是一份商业计划书），再到制定预算、为预算筹资、管理捐赠者，以及围绕一个愿景和想法来聚集人们。

00:10:02.680 --> 00:10:27.700
<v Eric Mennel>Fellowship Associates set Watson up with a year-and-a-half-long residency at a church plant like the one he wanted to create in a poor neighborhood in North Philadelphia, where Watson learned the nuts and bolts of a daily church operation. One of the main things to accomplish during the residency was working out a business plan. And I mean that literally-- a business plan, a clear idea for his church that he could pitch to raise money, with a clear, simple mission.
<v Eric Mennel>“团契同工”为沃森安排了为期一年半的驻点实习，地点在费城北部一个贫困社区的教会植堂项目，就像他想创办的那种教会一样。沃森在那里学习了教会日常运作的具体细节。在实习期间要完成的主要事情之一是制定商业计划。我的意思是字面上的——一份商业计划，一个他可以向人推介以筹集资金的清晰构思，以及一个清晰、简单的使命。

00:10:27.700 --> 00:10:34.890
<v Watson Jones>We want to create a church that will seek to reach people who don't know Jesus Christ or who are not a part of a church.
<v Watson Jones>我们要创办一个教会，旨在接触那些不认识耶稣基督或不属于任何教会的人。

00:10:34.890 --> 00:10:55.970
<v Eric Mennel>Part one of the pitch-- location. He'd stick with his vision to serve the kind of neighborhood church planters don't usually go into. For decades, when church painters went into urban neighborhoods, they usually picked gentrifying ones or wealthier ones. Watson chose an area on Philly's northwest side that was not gentrifying. It was mostly black, mostly low income.
<v Eric Mennel>推介的第一部分——地点。他将坚持自己的愿景，服务于教会植堂者通常不去的那种社区。几十年来，当教会植堂者进入城市社区时，他们通常选择士绅化或更富裕的社区。沃森选择了费城西北部一个没有士绅化的地区。那里主要是黑人，主要是低收入人群。

00:10:55.970 --> 00:11:25.230
<v Watson Jones>I am a product of the South Side of Chicago. My whole life has been living and navigating as a black Chicagoan on the South Side. That was core to me. So I really wanted to target the thoroughly urban Philadelphian, to engage people who Jesus would engage. Jesus was willing to be associated with prostitutes and with tax collectors.
<v Watson Jones>我是芝加哥南区的产物。我的整个人生都是在芝加哥南区作为一名黑人生活和导航。这对我很重要。所以我真的想把目标锁定在彻底的城市费城人身上，去接触耶稣会接触的人。耶稣愿意与妓女和税吏交往。

00:11:25.230 --> 00:11:49.080
<v Watson Jones>I had the idea of whosoever, we'll let them come. Whoever wants to be a part of our church can be a part of our church. Then I also wanted to be able to reach people that were in my age group initially-- at the time, I was 28, 29, 30-- and then also trying to reach older people-- my mother's generation, which is a little more stable, so--
<v Watson Jones>我有一个想法，就是“凡愿意的人”，让他们来。任何想成为我们教会一部分的人都可以加入。然后我还想先接触我这个年龄段的人——当时我28、29、30岁——同时也试着接触年长的人——我母亲那一代，他们比较稳定，所以——

00:11:49.080 --> 00:12:09.490
<v Eric   Mennel>So that was part one of the pitch-- location. Part two of the pitch-- the building, or lack thereof. They wouldn't have a building. This is a strategy that had been really successful for planting churches elsewhere, in well-off or gentrifying neighborhoods. They don't meet in an actual church.
<v Eric   Mennel>这就是推介的第一部分——地点。推介的第二部分——建筑物，或者说缺乏建筑物。他们不会有建筑物。这是一种在其他地方（在富裕或士绅化社区）建立教会非常成功的策略。他们不在真正的教会里聚会。

00:12:09.490 --> 00:12:24.020
<v Eric   Mennel>The idea is that the church buildings carry a lot of cultural baggage. They can be stodgy and uncomfortable. So to get away from all that, and as a sort of hack to save money early on, church plants will scrap the building. In most places, this is church planting 101.
<v Eric   Mennel>这个想法是教会建筑物承载了很多文化负担。它们可能死板且不舒服。为了摆脱这一切，也作为一种省钱的技巧，教会植堂会放弃建筑物。在大多数地方，这是教会植堂入门知识。

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:36.130
<v Watson Jones>I said, man, churches spend too much time focusing on a building. And they put so much money into a building. So early, early, early on, I wasn't thinking anything about owning a building.
<v Watson Jones>我说，老兄，教会花太多时间在建筑物上了。他们把太多的钱投进建筑物里。所以很早很早很早以前，我就没想过拥有建筑物的事。

00:12:36.130 --> 00:12:44.970
<v Eric Mennel>He figured they could meet in a coffee shop, or a bookstore, or something, even his living room. And finally, part three of the pitch-- a business partner.
<v Eric Mennel>他认为他们可以在咖啡店、书店或某个地方聚会，甚至是他的客厅。最后，推介的第三部分——商业伙伴。

00:12:44.970 --> 00:12:47.940
<v AJ Smith>No, OK, I'm going to give you a picture of myself at this point, OK? You ready?
<v AJ Smith>不，好吧，我现在给你描述一下当时的自己，好吗？准备好了吗？

00:12:47.940 --> 00:12:49.130
<v Eric Mennel>This is AJ Smith.
<v Eric Mennel>这是 AJ·史密斯（AJ Smith）。

00:12:49.130 --> 00:13:02.050
<v AJ Smith>I'm this white kid with dreadlocks all the way down my back, with a scraggly beard, wearing moccasins with holes in them, pants that have been patched up a million times, flannels ripped up.
<v AJ Smith>我是一个白人孩子，留着长到背后的脏辫，留着乱蓬蓬的胡子，穿着破了洞的鹿皮鞋，穿着打了几百万次补丁的裤子，穿着撕烂的法兰绒衬衫。

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:10.720
<v Watson Jones>Now, AJ, a good day for him is to go sit in the mountains and just sit there by himself. That's a good day for him. That's kind of how he dressed.
<v Watson Jones>现在，对 AJ 来说，美好的一天是去山上，一个人坐着。这对他就很好了。他穿衣服就是那样的。

00:13:10.720 --> 00:13:12.370
<v Eric Mennel>Crunchy dude.
<v Eric Mennel>一个特立独行的家伙。

00:13:12.370 --> 00:13:14.760
<v Watson Jones>Yeah, no, yeah. But he was a nice guy.
<v Watson Jones>是的，不，是的。但他是个好人。

00:13:14.760 --> 00:13:24.830
<v Eric Mennel>AJ actually brought a lot more than just worn-out moccasins to the table. He was training at a church nearby to plant a church in the inner city. He's 31 now, and he had grown up in a church plant.
<v Eric Mennel>AJ 带来的远不止破烂的鹿皮鞋。他正在附近的一家教会接受培训，计划在市中心建立一个教会。他现在31岁，是在一个教会植堂项目中长大的。

00:13:24.830 --> 00:13:34.560
<v Eric Mennel>He'd gone to seminary. He'd run a homeless ministry. Once you got past the dreadlocks, AJ seemed like a great number-two guy. And he was fired up to help make Watson's vision a reality.
<v Eric Mennel>他去过神学院。他经营过一个无家可归者事工。一旦你忽略了他的脏辫，AJ 看起来就像个很棒的二号人物。他热衷于帮助沃森将愿景变为现实。

00:13:34.560 --> 00:13:48.970
<v AJ Smith>I really believe strongly in submitting to African-American leadership, if you're in a largely minority setting-- or minority leadership, even. So I'm this white guy. So I say, yeah, man. I love the idea of, like-- I want to be in the inner city. I want to plant churches. Let me spend some time under you and just helping you do your thing.
<v AJ Smith>我真的强烈认为，如果你在一个主要是少数族裔的环境中，应该顺服非裔美国人的领导——甚至是少数族裔的领导。所以我这个白人，我会说，是的，伙计。我喜欢这个想法，我想在市中心。我想建立教会。让我留在你手下，帮你做你的事。

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:50.970
<v AJ Smith>Talked with my wife. We were like, yeah. I like it. Let's do it.
<v AJ Smith>和我的妻子谈过了。我们觉得，是的。我喜欢它。我们干吧。

00:13:50.970 --> 00:13:56.860
<v Watson Jones>We prayed about it. And we felt like it was what the lord wanted.
<v Watson Jones>我们为此祈祷。我们觉得这就是主想要的。

00:13:56.860 --> 00:13:59.370
<v AJ Smith>It was going to be called Restoration Church.
<v AJ Smith>它将被命名为“复兴教会”（Restoration Church）。

00:13:59.370 --> 00:14:15.890
<v Eric Mennel>Watson didn't have the direct connections to megachurches who might help fund Restoration. But he'd heard about some people who did-- a church planting network called Orchard Group. Orchard is this middleman organization. They help connect church plants to established churches that have a lot of money.
<v Eric Mennel>沃森没有直接联系到可能帮助资助复兴教会的大型教会。但他听说过一些有门路的人——一个叫“果园小组”（Orchard Group）的教会植堂网络。果园小组是中间人组织。他们帮助将教会植堂项目与资金雄厚的成熟教会联系起来。

00:14:15.890 --> 00:14:31.710
<v Eric Mennel>Orchard Group is very selective. Of the thousands of people planting churches every year, about 100 apply with them, and the network only supports three or four. Watson called the president of the network directly. And before even putting in a formal application, they were excited about his vision.
<v Eric Mennel>果园小组非常有选择性。每年有成千上万的人建立教会，约有100人向他们申请，而该网络只支持三四个。沃森直接给该网络的负责人打了电话。在甚至还没有提交正式申请之前，他们就对他的愿景感到兴奋。

00:14:31.710 --> 00:14:58.960
<v Eric Mennel>So in 2013, Restoration Church became one of the few churches they supported. They helped line up a good chunk of startup capital-- in total, about $100,000 per year for three years. Most of that was just going to cover his salary and half of AJ's. The reason for the three-year funding terms is that surveys of new churches show, if a church plant isn't sustainable after three years, it will likely never be sustainable. And nobody wants to sink their money into a failing church.
<v Eric Mennel>所以2013年，复兴教会成为他们支持的少数教会之一。他们帮助筹集了一大笔启动资金——总共约为每年10万美元，持续三年。其中大部分只是为了覆盖他的薪水和 AJ 的一半薪水。三年资助期限的原因是，对新教会的调查显示，如果一个教会植堂项目在三年后无法维持，它可能永远无法维持。没有人愿意把钱投入一个注定失败的教会。

00:14:58.960 --> 00:15:16.350
<v Eric Mennel>And so, with their seed funding in place, the countdown started. It was April 2014, and Watson and AJ had three years to get this church off the ground, to attract enough people to become self-sustaining.
<v Eric Mennel>于是，随着种子资金到位，倒计时开始了。那是2014年4月，沃森和 AJ 有三年时间让这个教会起步，吸引足够多的人实现自负盈亏。

00:15:16.350 --> 00:15:26.120
<v Eric Mennel>Initially, they just wanted people to come to a once-a-week Bible study at Watson's home. So the first thing they did was try to drum up interest the old-fashioned way-- through outreach on the streets in the neighborhood.
<v Eric Mennel>起初，他们只是想让人来沃森家参加每周一次的圣经学习。所以他们做的第一件事就是试图以老式的方式——通过在社区街道上进行外展活动——来激发兴趣。

00:15:26.120 --> 00:15:34.010
<v Watson Jones>Early on, man, me and AJ were out on corners, passing out coffee, free coffee, on bus stops.
<v Watson Jones>早期，伙计，我和 AJ 在街角分发咖啡，免费咖啡，在公交车站。

00:15:34.010 --> 00:15:38.930
<v AJ Smith>We would make signs. We'd go to the community days. We'd go to volunteer at elementary schools.
<v AJ Smith>我们会制作标志。我们会参加社区日活动。我们会去小学做志愿者。

00:15:38.930 --> 00:15:45.110
<v Watson Jones>We passed out water. We would have our team standing on crowded corners in the hot summer.
<v Watson Jones>我们分发水。我们的团队会在炎热的夏天站在拥挤的街角。

00:15:45.110 --> 00:15:49.390
<v Leah Smith>We were handing out Blow Pops to people.
<v Leah Smith>我们正在给人们分发 Blow Pops 棒棒糖。

00:15:49.390 --> 00:15:51.910
<v Eric Mennel>This is Leah Smith, AJ's wife.
<v Eric Mennel>这是莉亚·史密斯（Leah Smith），AJ 的妻子。

00:15:51.910 --> 00:16:04.650
<v Leah Smith>And I think we put a little message on them-- let the love of God blow you away-- so corny. So corny. But we were trying to get people. It kind of felt like anything could happen at any moment.
<v Leah Smith>我觉得我们在上面写了点信息——让神的爱让你震撼——太俗气了。太俗气了。但我们是在试图吸引人。感觉好像随时都可能发生任何事情。

00:16:04.650 --> 00:16:17.740
<v Watson Jones>So as we meet with people, we're going to ask, hey, man, who are three to four people you think would be interested in this? And we met a ton of people, a lot of people. We prayed for and prayed with a lot of people.
<v Watson Jones>所以当我们和人们见面时，我们会问，嘿，伙计，你觉得有哪三到四个人会对这个感兴趣？我们见到了很多人，很多人。我们为很多人祈祷，也与很多人一起祈祷。

00:16:17.740 --> 00:16:27.400
<v Eric Mennel>Over the course of just a few months, they had conversations with hundreds of people. And occasionally, some of them would take down Watson's address and show up to the Bible study.
<v Eric Mennel>在短短几个月的时间里，他们与数百人进行了交谈。偶尔，其中一些人会记下沃森的地址并出现在圣经学习中。

00:16:27.400 --> 00:16:29.850
<v Leah Smith>Any time a new person would come, it was like, ah, this is exciting! This is great!
<v Leah Smith>任何时候有新人来，感觉就像，啊，这太令人兴奋了！这太棒了！

00:16:29.850 --> 00:16:38.360
<v Watson Jones>We had interest from all kinds of people-- people who didn't go to church, people who did go to church were very interested in us, because we looked different. And they liked us. They liked our spirit.
<v Watson Jones>我们吸引了各种各样的人——不去教会的人，去教会的人对我们非常感兴趣，因为我们看起来不一样。他们喜欢我们。他们喜欢我们的精神。

00:16:38.360 --> 00:16:54.370
<v AJ Smith>For the most part, people were very receptive, and even, I'd say, very respectful of the ministry and what we were trying to do, and were very appreciative. But it didn't translate. We largely were not successful at getting people to come to our outreaches. It was a major disappointment.
<v AJ Smith>在大多数情况下，人们非常接受，甚至可以说，非常尊重这个事工和我们试图做的事情，也非常感激。但它没有转化。我们在吸引人们参加外展活动方面基本是不成功的。这是一个重大的失望。

00:16:54.370 --> 00:16:59.730
<v Watson Jones>Man, I probably gained 20 pounds that year alone. It was extremely stressful.
<v Watson Jones>伙计，那一年我可能胖了20磅。压力极大。

00:16:59.730 --> 00:17:30.080
<v Eric  Mennel>It felt like the outreaches were missing the mark. The goal had always been to reach people who weren't served by other churches, who weren't going to church. But by the end of six months, it was clear-- the people showing up, for the most part, were already church people coming from other churches. And they realized, maybe one of their basic premises was wrong. Something that other church plants always did, something that worked in the suburbs, was not working here.
<v Eric  Mennel>感觉外展活动没有达到目标。目标一直是接触那些未被其他教会服务的、不去教会的人。但在六个月结束时，很清楚——出现的人在很大程度上已经是来自其他教会的教会人员。他们意识到，也许他们的一些基本前提是错误的。其他教会植堂项目总是做的、在郊区有效的事情，在这里行不通。

00:17:30.080 --> 00:17:41.730
<v Watson  Jones>I think, in retrospect, what probably hurt us is, the thing to bring them to was a Bible study in my house. The first thing they'd ask me is, where is your church? Oh, well, we meet in my house.
<v Watson  Jones>我认为，回想起来，可能伤害我们的是，带他们来的地方是我家里的圣经学习班。他们问我的第一件事是，你的教会在哪里？噢，好吧，我们在我家聚会。

00:17:41.730 --> 00:17:57.410
<v Watson  Jones>And one lady told me-- she said, you guys are a cult. You call me when you get a church. Especially, I think, among black people, the more out of the box or avant garde you are, the less likely you are to be trusted.
<v Watson  Jones>有一位女士告诉我——她说，你们是个邪教。等你们有了教堂再给我打电话。我认为，特别是在黑人中，你越是不按常理出牌或前卫，人们越不信任你。

00:17:57.410 --> 00:18:13.610
<v Watson  Jones>Theologically, we say all day long, the church is the people of God. The people in your city, in your neighborhood, does not understand church apart from a building, a preacher, a choir or a praise team, and something that looks like a church service, period.
<v Watson  Jones>在神学上，我们整天说，教会就是神的子民。你所在的城市、你所在的社区里的人，如果不理解教会是离不开建筑物、传教士、唱诗班或赞美团队，以及看起来像教会礼拜的那种东西，周期性地（就是这样）。

00:18:13.610 --> 00:18:22.240
<v AJ Smith>That was really a gut check to us, because that was our-- that was plan A. And we weren't really sure what plan B was.
<v AJ Smith>这对我们来说真是一个直觉测试，因为那是我们的——那是计划 A。我们真的不确定计划 B 是什么。

00:18:22.240 --> 00:18:23.570
<v Eric Mennel>Plan A was the outreaches?
<v Eric Mennel>计划 A 是外展活动？

00:18:23.570 --> 00:18:34.440
<v AJ Smith>Yeah. I mean, we were going to be the people who were out there on the streets, pastors who were very much present with the people. And that's how we'll grow the church. That didn't work.
<v AJ Smith>是的。我是说，我们要成为那些在街头上的人，牧师要与人们真正在一起。这就是我们将如何发展教会的方式。那行不通。

00:18:34.440 --> 00:18:37.640
<v Eric Mennel>Hm. Why not?
<v Eric Mennel>嗯。为什么不行？

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:54.940
<v Aj  Smith>I think people have been to church. I think people have done church. And I think people don't have great experiences with church. And because of that, I think the last thing people want to do is waste a day, in their mind, of the weekend coming to church.
<v Aj  Smith>我认为人们去过教会。我认为人们做过教会。而且我认为人们在教会里并没有很好的经历。正因为如此，我认为人们最不想做的事情就是在他们的周末浪费一天时间来教会。

00:18:54.940 --> 00:18:56.470
<v Eric  Mennel>Yeah.
<v Eric  Mennel>是啊。

00:18:56.470 --> 00:19:11.970
<v AJ Smith>I love what you all are doing, but you know-- OK, maybe I'll come by sometime. They ain't coming to church. They've been to church. Their uncle started a church 20 years ago, and they had to go sit through three hours on a Sunday morning, couldn't wait to get out of there. They couldn't wait till they were 18, and they didn't have to go to church anymore.
<v AJ Smith>我喜欢你们所做的一切，但你知道——好吧，也许我以后会来看看。他们才不会来教会呢。他们去过教会。他们的叔叔20年前创办了一个教会，他们必须在周日上午坐在那里三个小时，迫不及待地想离开。他们等不及要等到18岁，这样他们就不必再去了。

00:19:11.970 --> 00:19:40.440
<v Eric   Mennel>This is perhaps where planting a church is most different from starting a company. It is very hard to get someone to try a new app. But what Watson was attempting seems even harder-- to get someone to try something they've already tried and rejected, and then to readjust their whole way of understanding the universe and their feelings about God, and to start going to church, and to help fund that church.
<v Eric   Mennel>这也许就是植堂与创办公司最不同的地方。让某人尝试一个新的应用程序是非常困难的。但沃森所尝试的似乎更难——让某人尝试他们已经尝试过并拒绝的东西，然后重新调整他们看待宇宙的全部方式和对神的感受，并开始去教会，并帮助资助那个教会。

00:19:40.440 --> 00:19:57.260
<v Eric   Mennel>It's exactly the problem the church planting movement is supposed to solve, and does solve in lots of new churches. But the way most church planters usually solve this problem is to make church feel more relevant, meet in weirder spaces, like warehouses or coffee shops. There are church plants in breweries.
<v Eric   Mennel>这正是教会植堂运动应该解决的问题，并且在许多新教会中确实解决了。但大多数植堂者通常解决这个问题的方法是让教会感觉更相关，在奇怪的空间里聚会，比如仓库或咖啡店。有些植堂项目在酿酒厂里。

00:19:57.260 --> 00:20:08.110
<v Eric   Mennel>The music in a typical church plant does not sound like the music in a traditional church. There are electric guitars with lots of effects on them. If they sing hymns, they're done in a style more like Mumford and Sons than Mozart.
<v Eric   Mennel>典型的教会植堂项目中的音乐听起来不像传统教会中的音乐。有带有很多效果的电吉他。如果他们唱赞美诗，其风格更像是蒙福之子乐团（Mumford and Sons）而不是莫扎特。

00:20:08.110 --> 00:20:09.970
<v Eric   Mennel>[MUSIC - "I WILL WAIT" BY MUMFORD AND SONS]
<v Eric   Mennel>[音乐 - 蒙福之子乐团的《I Will Wait》]

00:20:09.970 --> 00:20:10.980
<v Marcus Mumford>(SINGING) And I will--
<v Marcus Mumford>(演唱) 我会——

00:20:10.980 --> 00:20:21.100
<v Eric Mennel>This is a church plant worship service where they're literally just playing Mumford and Sons. And I got to say, the lyrics, "I will wait for you," land very differently when you're thinking about the rapture.
<v Eric Mennel>这是一个教会植堂崇拜仪式，他们实际上只是在播放蒙福之子乐团的音乐。我不得不说，当你想到被提（rapture）时，歌词“我会为你等待”的含义就完全不同了。

00:20:21.100 --> 00:20:29.400
<v Marcus  Mumford>(SINGING) I will wait. I will wait for you. I will wait. I will wait--
<v Marcus  Mumford>(演唱) 我会等待。我会为你等待。我会等待。我会等待——

00:20:29.400 --> 00:20:49.990
<v Eric  Mennel>These churches are intentionally made to feel like going to a concert, not like going to church. There are professional lighting systems, and even smoke machines in some cases. But the thing about relevancy is that it's relative. A lot of that stuff-- the lights, the music-- it was designed by white people for white people.
<v Eric  Mennel>这些教会故意让人感觉像去听音乐会，而不是去教会。有专业的照明系统，甚至在某些情况下还有烟雾机。但关于相关性的问题在于它是相对的。很多东西——灯光、音乐——都是白人设计的，为了白人。

00:20:49.990 --> 00:21:14.850
<v Eric  Mennel>What Watson and AJ were finding was that, in an African-American context in northwest Philly, those things don't necessarily feel relevant. So nine months in, Watson and AJ decided to scrap a key part of their business plan, one of the things that was going to make their church so different. And that's the part about having no building. They decided to reboot and to hold regular weekly services in a building, like the neighborhood seemed to want.
<v Eric  Mennel>沃森和 AJ 发现的是，在费城西北部的非裔美国人语境中，这些东西不一定让人觉得相关。所以9个月后，沃森和 AJ 决定废除他们商业计划的关键部分，即那些使他们的教会如此与众不同的东西之一。那就是关于没有建筑物的部分。他们决定重启，并像社区似乎希望的那样，在一个建筑物里举行定期的每周礼拜。

00:21:14.850 --> 00:21:27.930
<v Eric  Mennel>They did two preview services-- preview services are a kind of beta test for the church-- in a banquet hall. They wanted to pick a space that didn't carry any of the negative connotations and baggage of a traditional church. But it was maybe a bit of an overcorrection.
<v Eric  Mennel>他们做了两次预览礼拜——预览礼拜是教会的一种 Beta 测试——在宴会厅里。他们想选择一个不带有传统教会负面内涵和包袱的空间。但这可能有点矫枉过正。

00:21:27.930 --> 00:21:32.460
<v Watson Jones>We went to this place called Temptations. And we went to Temptations--
<v Watson Jones>我们去了这个叫“诱惑”（Temptations）的地方。我们去了诱惑——

00:21:32.460 --> 00:21:35.230
<v Eric Mennel>Wait, it's called Temptations?
<v Eric Mennel>等等，它叫“诱惑”？

00:21:35.230 --> 00:21:46.110
<v Watson Jones>Yeah, yeah, we-- yeah, we made all kind of jokes about that. Yeah, Temptations. The first time we went there, they just had trash everywhere-- hair weaves, and bras, and open bottles, and--
<v Watson Jones>是的，是的，我们——是的，我们开了各种关于那个名字的玩笑。是的，诱惑。我们第一次去那里的时候，那里到处都是垃圾——发织、胸罩、开封的瓶子，还有——

00:21:46.110 --> 00:21:48.020
<v Eric Mennel>Wait, there were literally, like, bras?
<v Eric Mennel>等等，那里真的有胸罩？

00:21:48.020 --> 00:21:56.170
<v Watson Jones>Yeah, yeah. We saw two bras, just on the floor, while trying to set our own stuff up.
<v Watson Jones>是的，是的。我们就在那，试图布置我们自己的东西时，在地板上看到了两个胸罩。

00:21:56.170 --> 00:22:18.340
<v Eric   Mennel>So next, they tried an elementary school cafeteria. That's where they moved out of beta and held their first-ever regular Sunday service. In the church planting world, it's called launch Sunday. And it's a huge deal for a church plant-- a sort flag-on-the-moon moment saying, we are here to stay.
<v Eric   Mennel>接下来，他们尝试了一个小学的食堂。那是他们结束 Beta 测试并举行第一次常规周日礼拜的地方。在教会植堂界，这被称为“启动周日”。这对教会植堂来说是一件大事——一种在月球上插旗的时刻，宣布我们已经扎根了。

00:22:18.340 --> 00:22:30.790
<v Eric   Mennel>It was March 2015, 11 months after they started, just about two years left on the countdown clock. They put out the word on Facebook, on flyers. They told family and friends. And then they prayed.
<v Eric   Mennel>那是2015年3月，他们开始11个月后，倒计时钟上只剩下两年了。他们在 Facebook 上发布了消息，印了传单。他们告诉了家人和朋友。然后他们祈祷。

00:22:30.790 --> 00:22:34.710
<v Eric  Mennel>Were you nervous before?
<v Eric  Mennel>你之前紧张吗？

00:22:34.710 --> 00:22:38.530
<v Watson  Jones>Very, yeah. Very nervous, yes.
<v Watson  Jones>非常紧张，是的。非常紧张，是的。

00:22:38.530 --> 00:22:41.180
<v Eric Mennel>What was going through your mind?
<v Eric Mennel>你当时在想什么？

00:22:41.180 --> 00:22:52.480
<v Watson Jones>What if no one comes? Why would they come? There was a guy I knew who had launched a church. And every week, it decreased until, like, a month later, it didn't exist anymore.
<v Watson Jones>如果没人来怎么办？他们为什么要来？我认识一个创办过教会的家伙。每周都在减少，直到一个月后，它就不复存在了。

00:22:52.480 --> 00:23:05.530
<v Watson Jones>So in my mind, I'm like, well, what if that's me? What do I tell these donors who gave a lot of money to this? And what will I tell my wife? What will I tell my family? All of that.
<v Watson Jones>所以在我的脑海里，我想，好吧，如果那是我呢？我该怎么告诉那些为这个捐了很多钱的捐赠者？我该怎么告诉我妻子？我该怎么告诉我的家人？所有这一切。

00:23:05.530 --> 00:23:07.410
<v Watson Jones>Amen. You may be seated in the presence of the lord.
<v Watson Jones>阿门。在主的面前，请坐。

00:23:07.410 --> 00:23:22.020
<v Eric Mennel>Watson woke up on the morning of March 22, 2015. He put on his brown corduroy pants, and a blue patterned shirt, and a white cardigan. And at 11:00 AM, he stepped up to the podium in front of more than 100 cafeteria chairs. And he started to pray.
<v Eric Mennel>2015年3月22日早晨，沃森醒来。他穿上棕色的灯芯绒裤子、一件蓝色图案衬衫和一件白色开衫。上午11点，他走上讲台，面对着100多把食堂椅子。他开始祈祷。

00:23:22.020 --> 00:23:32.330
<v Watson Jones>Lord, while we use technical terms of launching, and all of that stuff, Lord, we don't want any of all of this to stand in the way of you getting glory and you getting honor.
<v Watson Jones>主啊，虽然我们使用“启动”之类的技术术语，所有这些，主啊，我们不希望所有这些阻碍你得到荣耀和尊荣。

00:23:32.330 --> 00:23:34.770
<v Eric Mennel>What did the launch look like? How many people were there?
<v Eric Mennel>启动看起来是什么样子的？有多少人在场？

00:23:34.770 --> 00:23:39.880
<v Watson Jones>Man, I think it might have been maybe 150, maybe more.
<v Watson Jones>伙计，我想可能有150人，甚至更多。

00:23:39.880 --> 00:23:40.640
<v Eric Mennel>Wow.
<v Eric Mennel>哇。

