<v Watson  Jones>I don't know. I don't know.
<v Watson  Jones>我不知道。我不知道。

00:26:35.460 --> 00:26:45.200
<v Eric Mennel>There were periods over the next year where attendance was so low, one person told me it felt like the band was playing for itself. The hardest stretches for Watson?
<v Eric Mennel>接下来的那一年里，有几段时间出席率如此之低，一个人告诉我，感觉乐队就像在为自己演奏。沃森最艰难的阶段？

00:26:45.200 --> 00:27:15.130
<v Watson  Jones>Summer months. Summer months is when everybody in Philadelphia went to the shore, you know? Or everybody traveled. And so attendance-wise, man, it felt like someone took a scalpel and cut massive chunks off the church. Those periods were extremely deflating. I mean, they were dark moments.
<v Watson  Jones>夏季。夏天是费城每个人都去海边的时候，你知道吗？或者每个人都在旅行。所以出席率方面，伙计，感觉就像有人拿手术刀切掉了教会的一大块。那些阶段极其令人沮丧。我的意思是，那是非常黑暗的时刻。

00:27:15.130 --> 00:27:31.970
<v Watson  Jones>Sometimes, I would battle depression. Most preachers tend to take Monday off, because you tend to be more prone to depression on a Monday, because Sunday, you expend so much of yourself, you know? You're preaching, and then you're talking to people. You're counseling people. You have meetings and all that other stuff.
<v Watson  Jones>有时，我会与抑郁作斗争。大多数传教士倾向于在周一休息，因为你在周一更容易倾向于抑郁，因为周日，你耗费了太多的自己，你知道吗？你在布道，然后你在和人交谈。你在辅导人。你有会议和所有其他的事情。

00:27:31.970 --> 00:27:45.880
<v Watson  Jones>But for a church planter in an urban church struggling to move this thing, it was exponentially worse. You're tired. You're busy. You're lonely. You're burned out.
<v Watson  Jones>但对于一个在城市教会中努力推动这件事的植堂者来说，情况呈指数级恶化。你累了。你很忙。你很孤独。你精疲力竭。

00:27:45.880 --> 00:28:06.110
<v Watson  Jones>I would wonder, was this God, or was this me? Did God really call me to Philly, or did he not? I felt like quitting, because any church planter I would ever talk to would say that they felt that at some point.
<v Watson  Jones>我曾想，这是神的意思，还是我自己的意思？神真的呼召我去费城了吗，还是没有？我觉得想放弃，因为我交谈过的任何教会植堂者都会说他们在某个时刻也有这种感觉。

00:28:06.110 --> 00:28:21.860
<v Eric  Mennel>Nearly two years went by like this. They'd get up to 60 people, and then they'd drop again. They were coming up on March 2017, their three-year deadline.
<v Eric  Mennel>就这样过了近两年。他们会增加到60人，然后又下降。他们快要到2017年3月了，他们的三年最后期限。

00:28:21.860 --> 00:28:38.790
<v Eric  Mennel>It's not that you can't plant a church in a neighborhood like this and succeed. The church that Watson was a resident at, run by a pastor named Dr. Eric Mason-- it's nearly 12 years old now, with hundreds of attendees every week. Another preacher, named Doug Logan, has done something similar in Camden, New Jersey.
<v Eric  Mennel>并不是说你不能在这样的社区里建立教会并成功。沃森曾驻点的那个教会，由埃里克·梅森（Dr. Eric Mason）博士经营——现在已经近12年了，每周有数百人参加。另一位叫道格·洛根（Doug Logan）的传教士在新泽西州的卡姆登也做了类似的事情。

00:28:38.790 --> 00:28:56.760
<v Eric  Mennel>But these guys, the Erics and the Dougs-- they are machines. They are not like normal people. They write books and organize conferences. They have web series. It's exactly the kind of mentality you imagine in a successful startup CEO. Watson was a talented guy with a lot of energy. But he wasn't that.
<v Eric  Mennel>但这些家伙，埃里克和道格——他们是机器。他们不像普通人。他们写书，组织会议。他们有网络系列。这完全是你想象中一个成功的创业 CEO 的心态。沃森是个有才华、精力充沛的家伙。但他不是那样的人。

00:28:56.760 --> 00:29:11.700
<v Watson Jones>A lot of the times, I felt it was something about me. Maybe I'm doing something wrong. Maybe I'm missing something. This is not happening because you're just not a great leader, or you're not a great preacher, or you're not a great whatever, whatever, whatever. I don't know.
<v Watson Jones>很多时候，我觉得这跟我自己有关。也许我做错了什么。也许我错过了一些什么。这之所以没有发生，是因为你不是一个伟大的领袖，或者你不是一个伟大的传教士，或者你不是一个伟大的什么什么什么。我不知道。

00:29:11.700 --> 00:29:12.260
<v Eric Mennel>Yeah.
<v Eric Mennel>是啊。

00:29:12.260 --> 00:29:13.940
<v Watson  Jones>I don't know.
<v Watson  Jones>我不知道。

00:29:13.940 --> 00:29:20.300
<v Eric Mennel>On top of all this, Watson and his wife were dealing with all kinds of complicated family stuff.
<v Eric Mennel>除此之外，沃森和他的妻子还在处理各种复杂的家庭事务。

00:29:20.300 --> 00:29:45.120
<v Watson Jones>We had a miscarriage before my third child, and that was very painful. We had a diagnosis of autism on my first child. And in that same period, while starting a church, my wife almost died while giving birth to my final child. And all of that was a lot of trauma. I think it probably eventually made me wonder, is my time up?
<v Watson Jones>在我的第三个孩子之前，我们流产了，那非常痛苦。我们的大孩子被诊断出患有自闭症。而在同一时期，在开始教会的同时，我的妻子在生我的最后一个孩子时差点死了。所有这一切都是巨大的创伤。我想这可能最终让我怀疑，我的时间到了吗？

00:29:45.120 --> 00:30:05.440
<v Eric  Mennel>Time was up. The three-year clock ran down. They still had some money coming in from their donors, and they were getting by. But Watson knew, some of those donors were going away. It wasn't going to be enough to pay both him and AJ. So last spring, Watson took a trip back home to Chicago, alone, to see his family.
<v Eric  Mennel>时间到了。三年倒计时结束了。他们仍然从捐赠者那里得到一些钱，并且还能应付。但沃森知道，其中一些捐赠者要离开了。这不足以同时支付他和 AJ 的薪水。所以去年春天，沃森独自回到了芝加哥的家看望家人。

00:30:05.440 --> 00:30:32.190
<v Watson Jones>That weekend was Mother's Day weekend, actually. And I was on a layover in Chicago and I got to see my mother, right? And my mother-- it was the first time I had been with her on a Mother's Day for some years-- like, five years. It was so special. And I just started to feel like, man, if I could not go back, I wouldn't go back.
<v Watson Jones>那个周末其实是母亲节周末。我在芝加哥中转，我见到了我的母亲，对吧？而我的母亲——这是我几年以来第一次在母亲节和她在一起——大约五年吧。那非常特别。我开始觉得，伙计，如果我能不回去，我就不会回去了。

00:30:32.190 --> 00:30:49.520
<v Watson Jones>I loved my church deeply. But I started to feel like the Lord was saying, it's time. So I talked to my wife about it. And I just said, look, I'm just going to tell you this. Don't get mad at me. But I'm just going to let you know exactly what I'm thinking here. And my wife said to me, you know, Watson, I've been feeling the same way.
<v Watson Jones>我深深地爱着我的教会。但我开始觉得主在说，时候到了。所以我跟我妻子谈了这件事。我只是对她说，看，我只想告诉你这件事。别生我的气。我只想让你知道我在这里的想法。我妻子对我说，你知道，沃森，我也有同样的感觉。

00:30:49.520 --> 00:31:02.490
<v Eric Mennel>And then, one Sunday morning last October, Watson got up in front of the congregation of Restoration. And he gave them a sermon that seemed like advice to prepare them for the road ahead.
<v Eric Mennel>然后，去年10月的一个周日上午，沃森站在复兴教会的会众面前。他发表了一篇布道，似乎是为他们的前路提供建议。

00:31:02.490 --> 00:31:10.540
<v Watson Jones>This is the part in your life where you cannot find your way. You don't know to go left. You don't know to go right. You don't know to go up. You don't know to go down.
<v Watson Jones>这是你生活中无法找到出路的时刻。你不知道该往左走。你不知道该往右走。你不知道该往上走。你不知道该往下走。

00:31:10.540 --> 00:31:20.930
<v Watson Jones>You don't know who to call. You don't know where to go. This is the in-between times. And in-between times, according to the biblical narrative, have the ability to either make you, or they can break you.
<v Watson Jones>你不知道给谁打电话。你不知道去哪里。这是中间的时光。而根据圣经叙事，中间的时光有能力要么成就你，要么摧毁你。

00:31:20.930 --> 00:31:33.290
<v Eric Mennel>After the service, he had all the members stay for a meeting, at which point he said he was officially stepping down and they'd be going home to Chicago with his family. Taking over the church, AJ.
<v Eric Mennel>礼拜结束后，他让所有成员留下来开会，在会上他说他正式辞职了，他们将和家人回芝加哥。接管教会的是 AJ。

00:31:33.290 --> 00:31:37.000
<v Leah Smith>My first-- my first reaction was no-- like, no, no.
<v Leah Smith>我的第一——我的第一反应是不——就像，不，不。

00:31:37.000 --> 00:31:39.410
<v Eric Mennel>AJ's wife, Leah.
<v Eric Mennel>AJ 的妻子，莉亚。

00:31:39.410 --> 00:31:44.460
<v Leah  Smith>No, you're not. I mean, majority African-American church. You're white.
<v Leah  Smith>不，你不是。我是说，以非裔美国人为主的教会。你是白人。

00:31:44.460 --> 00:31:56.730
<v Eric Mennel>To be clear, Leah's mother is black. But her father is white and a pastor for majority-black congregations. But, she says, AJ is really, really white.
<v Eric Mennel>需要澄清的是，莉亚的母亲是黑人。但她的父亲是白人，是多数黑人会众的牧师。但是，她说，AJ 确实非常非常白。

00:31:56.730 --> 00:32:10.020
<v Leah Smith>AJ-- he's not submerged in black culture. He's just not. I mean, it just was just weird to me. And I felt a little bit self-conscious about it, and still do.
<v Leah Smith>AJ——他没有沉浸在黑人文化中。他只是没有。我的意思是，这对我来说就是很奇怪。我对此感到有点拘束，现在仍然如此。

00:32:10.020 --> 00:32:33.600
<v Eric   Mennel>After moving back to Chicago, Watson looked around for another job. And he found himself drawn to exactly what he had been trying to disrupt-- an old church with a few hundred members and lots of years of baggage, Compassion Baptist Church. It was originally incorporated in 1879, two years after the end of Reconstruction.
<v Eric   Mennel>搬回芝加哥后，沃森四处寻找另一份工作。他发现自己被他一直试图破坏的东西吸引了——一个拥有几百名成员、背负着多年历史包袱的旧教会，慈悲浸信会（Compassion Baptist Church）。它最初成立于1879年，重建结束后两年。

00:32:33.600 --> 00:32:34.430
<v Eric   Mennel>[APPLAUSE]
<v Eric   Mennel>[掌声]

00:32:34.430 --> 00:32:37.200
<v Eric   Mennel>[CHEERING]
<v Eric   Mennel>[欢呼]

00:32:37.200 --> 00:32:50.470
<v Eric   Mennel>This is a recording from the morning Watson was installed as the new pastor there. He's onstage in a suit. Turns out, the look really works for him. And behind him is the church's choir-- which, alone, is almost as large as Restoration was when Watson left.
<v Eric   Mennel>这是沃森被任命为新牧师那天的录音。他穿着西装站在台上。事实证明，这个造型真的很适合他。在他身后是教会的唱诗班——仅此一项，就几乎和沃森离开时复兴教会的人数一样多。

00:32:50.470 --> 00:33:01.390
<v Watson Jones>At the church I'm at, for instance, the church is older than me. It's 138 years old. And my wife and I definitely brought the median age down some when we got there.
<v Watson Jones>我在现在的教会，例如，教会比我老。它有138年的历史。我和我妻子到那里时，确实把中位年龄降下来了一些。

00:33:01.390 --> 00:33:24.160
<v Watson Jones>But it's been interesting to see the life in that church with me being there, the new, young guy. Younger people are starting to come or come back. So it shows me that you can be effective in an established church. But seven years ago, I didn't believe that. Seven or eight years ago, I didn't believe that at all.
<v Watson Jones>但看到那个教会和我在一起生活的生命力，那个新的、年轻的家伙，非常有趣。年轻人开始来了，或者回来了。所以这向我证明，你可以在一个成熟的教会中发挥作用。但七年前，我不相信。七八年前，我根本不相信。

00:33:24.160 --> 00:33:46.370
<v Watson Jones>Some of those churches, man, survived the civil rights movement. Some of those churches brought black people in these cities through hellacious times. And then I, this little 26, 27, 28-year-old guy who just got a master's degree, wet behind the ears, I got the audacity to stand and critique something that's been around longer than me?
<v Watson Jones>那些教会中的一些，伙计，在民权运动中幸存了下来。那些教会中的一些引领了这些城市中的黑人度过了地狱般的时期。然后我，这个刚拿到硕士学位的26、27、28岁的毛头小子，乳臭未干，我竟然有胆量站出来评判一个比我存在时间还长的东西？

00:33:46.370 --> 00:33:49.800
<v Eric  Mennel>Yeah.
<v Eric  Mennel>是啊。

00:33:49.800 --> 00:33:51.710
<v Watson Jones>So my thoughts on that have changed.
<v Watson Jones>所以我在那方面的想法改变了。

00:33:51.710 --> 00:34:07.890
<v Eric Mennel>When Watson looks back at what went wrong at Restoration, there's a mix of things he comes back to. Partly, he thinks, it was him. He was a guy from Chicago trying to reach native Philadelphians. He had a big personality, but the planting process drained a lot of that out of him.
<v Eric Mennel>当沃森回顾复兴教会哪里出了问题时，他回想起了一些事情。部分原因，他认为，是他自己。他是一个来自芝加哥的家伙，试图接触费城的当地人。他有很大的个性，但植堂的过程抽干了其中的很多东西。

00:34:07.890 --> 00:34:26.960
<v Eric Mennel>But the real problem was probably their original premise-- to buck the conventional wisdom and locate in a neighborhood that wasn't gentrifying. People were already settled, stable. Now, Watson realizes just how much harder that made things. His intention was pure, but he didn't understand the difficulty of what he was setting out to do.
<v Eric Mennel>但真正的问题可能是他们最初的前提——试图对抗传统智慧，选址在没有士绅化的社区。人们已经安顿下来，很稳定。现在，沃森意识到他让事情变得有多难。他的意图是纯洁的，但他不明白他着手要做的事情的难度。

00:34:26.960 --> 00:34:47.100
<v Eric Mennel>If you want something to grow and be self-sustaining, this was not the place to do it. And the rest of the church gets that now. After Watson left, Restoration moved into a new neighborhood, one more in transition. And they bought a building. They're learning from their mistakes-- or anyway, they hope they are.
<v Eric Mennel>如果你想让某事成长并实现自负盈亏，这不是做这件事的地方。教会的其他人现在明白了。沃森离开后，复兴教会搬到了一个新的社区，一个更处于转型中的社区。他们买了一座建筑物。他们正在从错误中学习——或者无论如何，他们希望他们是。

00:34:47.100 --> 00:35:10.840
<v Ira Glass>Eric Mennel-- he tells the story of what happens after AJ takes over the church and moves it to a different area. That is the new season of the podcast StartUp. You can find StartUp at Apple Podcasts or gimletmedia.com, or wherever it is that you get your podcasts.
<v Ira Glass>埃里克·门内尔——他讲述了 AJ 接管教会并将其搬到不同地区后发生的故事。这是播客《StartUp》的新一季。你可以在 Apple Podcasts 或 gimletmedia.com，或者任何你获取播客的地方找到《StartUp》。

00:35:10.840 --> 00:35:36.360
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "I'M WORKING ON THE BUILDING" BY B.B. KING]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - B.B. King 的《I'm Working On The Building》]

00:35:36.360 --> 00:35:52.210
<v Ira Glass>Coming up, another newcomer arrives into a neighborhood. But instead of trying to get people to pray, her goal was simpler. She just wants them to drink. But attracting sinners can be just as tricky as attracting saints. That's in a minute from Chicago Public Radio when our program continues.
<v Ira Glass>接下来，另一位新来者抵达了一个社区。但她的目标不是试图让人祈祷，她的目标更简单。她只是想让他们喝酒。但吸引罪人可能和吸引圣徒一样棘手。从芝加哥公共广播电台，我们的节目继续。

00:35:52.210 --> 00:36:14.930
<v Ira Glass>It's This American Life. I'm Ira Glass. Each week on our program, of course, we choose a theme and bring you different kinds of stories on that theme. Today's show, If You Build It, Will They Come? Stories of people working and trying their hardest to create something, making mistakes they never thought could be mistakes. We've arrived at Act Two of our program-- Act Two, Hole in the Wall.
<v Ira Glass>这是《This American Life》。我是艾拉·格拉斯。每周在我们的节目中，当然，我们选择一个主题，并为您带来关于该主题的不同种类的故事。今天的节目，如果你盖好了，他们会来吗？人们努力工作并竭尽全力创造事物的故事，犯下他们从未想过可能是错误的错误。我们已经到了节目的第二幕——第二幕，墙上的洞。

00:36:14.930 --> 00:36:28.750
<v Ira Glass>So one of our coworkers here at the radio show, Neil Drumming, enjoys a drink now and then. And since he moved into his neighborhood a few years ago, he has visited just about every bar there, except one. He has stayed away. He'll explain why.
<v Ira Glass>我们电台的同事之一，尼尔·德拉明（Neil Drumming），偶尔喜欢喝一杯。自从几年前他搬进社区以来，他已经参观了那里几乎所有的酒吧，只有一个除外。他避而远之。他会解释原因。

00:36:28.750 --> 00:36:44.820
<v Ira Glass>And just a quick heads up here in the podcast that this story has some people in it who do not go to church, and we have un-beeped their ungodly cursing here in the podcast version of the show. If you want a beeped version-- maybe you're listening with kids, maybe you were interviewed in the first half of the program-- that version is at our website. Here's Neil.
<v Ira Glass>播客里提个醒，这个故事里有一些人不去教会，我们在播客版本的节目中没有把他们不敬神的诅咒消音。如果你想要消音版本——也许你在和孩子一起听，也许你是节目前半部分被采访的人——那个版本在我们网站上。这是尼尔。

00:36:44.820 --> 00:37:19.400
<v Neil Drumming>Last year, this restaurant in my neighborhood got into a lot of trouble with the locals. Summerhill, the restaurant, sits at the northeast corner of St. Marks and Nostrand Avenue in Crown Heights, a rapidly gentrifying area in Brooklyn. Basically, what happened was, in July of 2017, just after Summerhill opened to the public, the restaurant's owner, a white woman from Canada named Becca Brennan, put out a press release touting a few of her new venture's more unique characteristics-- surf club vibe, massive accordion window, and then, oddly, a quote, "bullet hole-ridden wall."
<v Neil Drumming>去年，我社区的这家餐馆给当地人惹了很大的麻烦。Summerhill 这家餐馆位于皇冠高地（Crown Heights）圣马可和诺斯特兰大道的东北角，这是一个正在迅速士绅化的布鲁克林地区。基本上发生的事情是，2017年7月，Summerhill 对公众开放后，餐馆的所有者，一位名叫贝卡·布伦南（Becca Brennan）的加拿大白人女性，发布了一份新闻稿，大肆宣扬她这一新创意的几个独特特征——冲浪俱乐部氛围、巨大的手风琴窗，然后，奇怪的是，还有一句引语：“布满弹孔的墙壁。”

00:37:19.400 --> 00:37:44.000
<v Neil Drumming>The release stated, yes, that bullet hole-ridden wall was originally there. And yes, we're keeping it. In an interview with the website Gothamist, Brennan suggested that this interior wall, speckled with paint and cracked plaster, was left over from a rumored back-room illegal gun shop. Brennan also told the reporter that Summerhill would be serving something called 40-ounce wine in paper bags.
<v Neil Drumming>发布声明称，是的，那面布满弹孔的墙原来就在那里。是的，我们保留它。在接受 Gothamist 网站采访时，布伦南暗示这面布满油漆和裂开石膏的内墙，是从一个传说中的非法枪支店后室留下的。布伦南还告诉记者，Summerhill 将提供一种叫“40盎司葡萄酒”的东西，放在纸袋里。

00:37:44.000 --> 00:38:05.950
<v Neil Drumming>This kind of thing happens so much in gentrifying Brooklyn that it's hardly notable anymore. Some trendy eatery, bar, or boutique flower shop will open where there used to be an old record store or a Caribbean takeout joint. And the owners will keep the crumbling facade, a faded sign, or a rusty light fixture visible, as if to say, hey, we love what used to be here. We're not here to change the neighborhood. We just want to be a part of it.
<v Neil Drumming>这种事情在正在士绅化的布鲁克林发生得太多了，以至于几乎不再引人注目。一些时髦的餐馆、酒吧或精品花店会开在曾经是旧唱片店或加勒比外卖店的地方。业主会保留破碎的门面、褪色的标志或生锈的灯具，仿佛在说，嘿，我们喜欢这里曾经的样子。我们不是来改变社区的。我们只是想成为其中的一部分。

00:38:05.950 --> 00:38:23.760
<v Neil Drumming>But in this case, Becca Brennan was indulging a bit of dark urban fantasy. The holes in the wall in question were not bullet holes, but actually anchor points for one of those big commercial refrigerators you find in any bodega. Summerhill had been completely remodeled from an old corner store that had once stood in that location.
<v Neil Drumming>但在这种情况下，贝卡·布伦南是在沉溺于一种黑暗的城市幻想。那面墙上的洞不是弹孔，而是你在任何便利店（bodega）都能找到的那种大商业冰箱的锚点。Summerhill 是从曾经位于那个位置的一家旧街角商店彻底改造而来的。

00:38:23.760 --> 00:38:40.140
<v Neil Drumming>Gothamist immediately ran a story about Summerhill's launch that included the interview with Brennan and a quote from a member of the Crown Heights Tenants Union accusing her of profiting from the neighborhood's unfortunate history of violence. TV news outlets and other websites, like Eater, picked up the story. And the next thing you know--
<v Neil Drumming>Gothamist 立即发表了一篇关于 Summerhill 开业的故事，包括对布伦南的采访，以及皇冠高地租户联盟成员的一句引语，指责她利用社区不幸的暴力历史牟利。电视新闻媒体和其他网站，如 Eater，报道了这个故事。接下来的事你知道——

00:38:40.140 --> 00:38:42.700
<v Woman>You ain't gonna take some motherfucking pain!
<v Woman>你不会拿走一些他妈的痛苦！

00:38:42.700 --> 00:38:46.630
<v Neil Drumming>--there was an angry mob standing outside Summerhill, protesting.
<v Neil Drumming>——有一个愤怒的暴徒站在 Summerhill 外面，抗议。

00:38:46.630 --> 00:38:50.220
<v Woman>You not gonna take our pain and make it a novelty!
<v Woman>你不会拿走我们的痛苦并把它变成新奇的东西！

00:38:50.220 --> 00:39:18.690
<v Neil Drumming>She said, "You're not going to take our pain and make it a novelty." This is from a video shot by Gothamist in July of last year. There were a lot of different kinds of people in the crowd outside of Summerhill, mostly young, presumably new to the neighborhood, certainly not all black. But the woman standing on the chair, yelling, is black. And when she shouts, "You're not going to take our pain and make it a novelty," she's talking about the pain of a predominantly African-American community that has long struggled with gun violence.
<v Neil Drumming>她说，“你不会拿走我们的痛苦并把它变成新奇的东西。”这是去年7月由 Gothamist 拍摄的一段视频。Summerhill 外面人群中有许多不同类型的人，大多是年轻人，想必是社区的新人，当然并非全是黑人。但站在椅子上大喊的那个女人是黑人。当她喊“你不会拿走我们的痛苦并把它变成新奇的东西”时，她谈论的是一个长期与枪支暴力斗争的非裔美国人社区的痛苦。

00:39:18.690 --> 00:39:44.800
<v Neil Drumming>I'd been living in Crown Heights less than a couple of years when this all went down. But when I moved here, one of the things I noticed were the signs-- the actual signs posted in storefronts near my apartment counting the days since the last shooting incident. Becca Brennan had chosen to make light of an issue that many people in the area took very seriously. And when a community meeting was called a month later to discuss it, what she said to the group just made things worse. She downplayed how much she'd talked about the bullet holes.
<v Neil Drumming>当这一切发生时，我在皇冠高地住了不到几年。但当我搬到这里时，我注意到的事情之一是标志——实际张贴在商店橱窗里、靠近我的公寓的标志，数着距离上次枪击事件过去的天数。贝卡·布伦南选择轻描淡写一个许多地区人民非常严肃对待的问题。而当一个月后召开社区会议讨论此事时，她对小组所说的话只会让事情变得更糟。她淡化了她谈论弹孔的次数。

00:39:44.800 --> 00:40:09.590
<v Becca Brennan>People would come in and say, are you keeping that wall? And I was-- I said yes. And some people would say, are those bullet holes? And I never once, to a person, said, yes, those are bullet holes. They are obviously holes from anchors in the wall. That's where the soda fridge was when the bodega was up, OK? So I'm sorry I have a sense of humor and--
<v Becca Brennan>人们进来问，你要保留那面墙吗？我当时是——我说是的。有些人会说，那是弹孔吗？我从来没有对任何一个人说过，是的，那是弹孔。它们显然是墙上的锚留下的洞。那就是以前那家便利店有汽水冰箱的地方，OK？所以我很抱歉我有幽默感和——

00:40:09.590 --> 00:40:27.400
<v Neil Drumming>That meeting got pretty heated. Brennan remained defensive and defiant as locals and their elected officials challenged her to apologize for her insensitivity. Some predicted that, without her contrition, Summerhill would soon shut down. Here's audio from another protest outside the restaurant, calling for just that.
<v Neil Drumming>那次会议非常激烈。布伦南保持防御和挑衅，当地人和他们的民选官员挑战她为她的麻木不仁道歉。一些人预测，如果没有她的忏悔，Summerhill 很快就会倒闭。这是餐馆外另一次抗议的录音，呼吁正是如此。

00:40:27.400 --> 00:40:30.310
<v Crowd>Bye-bye, Becky. Bye-bye, Becky. Bye-bye, Becky.
<v Crowd>再见，贝基。再见，贝基。再见，贝基。

00:40:30.310 --> 00:40:45.060
<v Neil Drumming>Becca Brennan didn't want to talk to us for this story. When Summerhill first opened, I had thought about stopping in for a drink a couple of times. But after I heard about the protest and skimmed the articles, staying clear felt like a no-brainer.
<v Neil Drumming>贝卡·布伦南不想为这个故事接受我们的采访。Summerhill 刚开业时，我曾想过进去喝一杯。但在听到抗议并浏览了文章后，远离它感觉是理所当然的。

00:40:45.060 --> 00:41:07.380
<v Neil Drumming>This conflict had two distinct sides. And even though I was pretty new to the neighborhood myself, there was no way I was going to align with the 31-year-old white gentrifier who fabricates a memorial to violence out of distressed concrete and an unconventional sense of humor. No question, I would stand with my people-- which, in a practical sense, just meant going instead to the bar across the street or the one around the corner.
<v Neil Drumming>这种冲突有两个明显的方面。即使我也是社区的新人，我也不可能与那个31岁的白人士绅化者站在一边，她用痛苦的混凝土和非传统的幽默感制造了一个针对暴力的纪念碑。毫无疑问，我会和我们自己人站在一起——这实际上意味着去街对面的酒吧或拐角处的酒吧。

00:41:07.380 --> 00:41:32.750
<v Neil Drumming>I didn't think about Summerhill for months. And then, less than a year later, it was 10 or 11 o'clock on a weekend, and I walked past the place. It was packed. The restaurant sits at an intersection and customers were spilling out onto the corner-- the same corner that had been overwhelmed with pissed-off protesters just last summer.
<v Neil Drumming>我有几个月没想过 Summerhill 了。然后，不到一年后，周末晚上10点或11点，我走过了那个地方。它挤满了人。餐馆位于十字路口，顾客溢到了拐角处——那个去年夏天被愤怒的抗议者淹没的同一个拐角处。

00:41:32.750 --> 00:41:46.630
<v Neil Drumming>Now, though, people inside and out seemed happy. They were drinking, partying, and it was really loud. I remember, because the DJ was playing something from Camp Lo's 1997 cult classic, Uptown Saturday Night. I love that album.
<v Neil Drumming>不过现在，里面和外面的人看起来都很开心。他们正在喝酒、聚会，声音非常大。我记得，因为 DJ 在播放 Camp Lo 1997年的邪典经典《Uptown Saturday Night》。我喜欢那张专辑。

00:41:46.630 --> 00:42:04.940
<v Neil Drumming>Oh, yeah, and pretty much everybody in the place was black. I felt like I'd missed the memo. Like, who ordered us to stand down? Not that I'd ever really stood up, but a lot of people had. I was at a bar across the street from Summerhill when I met a really friendly guy named Tashaka, who'd also been avoiding the crowds at Summerhill.
<v Neil Drumming>哦，是的，地方里几乎每个人都是黑人。我觉得我错过了备忘录。就像，谁命令我们退缩的？不是说我真的站出来过，但很多人都站出来过。我在 Summerhill 街对面的酒吧时，遇到了一个名叫塔沙卡（Tashaka）的非常友好的家伙，他也一直避开 Summerhill 的人群。

00:42:04.940 --> 00:42:13.650
<v Tashaka>But the reason I don't go in is because, initially, it was because she was-- they were being-- she was being boycotted and I didn't want to cross the line.
<v Tashaka>但我没进去的原因是，最初是因为她——他们正在——她正在被抵制，我不想跨越界限。

00:42:13.650 --> 00:42:15.080
<v Neil Drumming>So you didn't actually protest?
<v Neil Drumming>所以你其实没有抗议？

00:42:15.080 --> 00:42:15.570
<v Tashaka>Hm?
<v Tashaka>嗯？

00:42:15.570 --> 00:42:16.960
<v Neil Drumming>You didn't actually protest?
<v Neil Drumming>你其实没有抗议？

00:42:16.960 --> 00:42:22.790
<v Tashaka>Did I actually protest? Mm, I won't say-- I didn't-- I didn't stand outside.
<v Tashaka>我抗议了吗？嗯，我不会说——我没有——我没有站在外面。

00:42:22.790 --> 00:42:24.380
<v Neil Drumming>Yeah, me neither.
<v Neil Drumming>是啊，我也没。

00:42:24.380 --> 00:42:30.070
<v Neil Drumming>So why do you think that people started going after all? Why did black people decide--
<v Neil Drumming>所以你觉得为什么人们最后开始去了？为什么黑人决定——

00:42:30.070 --> 00:42:31.170
<v Tashaka>Because--
<v Tashaka>因为——

00:42:31.170 --> 00:42:32.680
<v Neil Drumming>--to start going?
<v Neil Drumming>——开始去？

00:42:32.680 --> 00:42:33.730
<v Tashaka>--we're very forgiving.
<v Tashaka>——我们非常宽容。

00:42:33.730 --> 00:42:35.220
<v Neil Drumming>Say it again?
<v Neil Drumming>再说一遍？

00:42:35.220 --> 00:42:49.070
<v Tashaka>We're very forgiving. It's like, you can smack us in the face, and if you say you're sorry and you're not going to do it again, we'll usually accept you. You know it's true. You're smiling, and you're giggling, but you know it's true.
<v Tashaka>我们非常宽容。就像，你可以扇我们脸，如果你说对不起，并且以后不再这样做，我们通常会接受你。你知道这是真的。你微笑着，你在傻笑，但你知道这是真的。

00:42:49.070 --> 00:43:08.020
<v Neil Drumming>For the record, I don't think I was giggling. Look, I take Tashaka's point. To put it lightly, African-Americans have been very forgiving about a lot of things over the years. But that wasn't a very satisfying answer. I just kind of wanted to know what really happened. So I walked across the street.
<v Neil Drumming>郑重声明，我不觉得我在傻笑。听着，我接受塔沙卡的观点。说得轻一点，多年来，非裔美国人在很多事情上一直非常宽容。但这并不是一个非常令人满意的答案。我只是想知道到底发生了什么。所以我走过了马路。

00:43:08.020 --> 00:43:10.040
<v Neil Drumming>And what made you come here?
<v Neil Drumming>是什么让你来到这儿？

00:43:10.040 --> 00:43:18.590
<v Woman 1>We were walking down Nostrand, and we were just trying to find somewhere to have a quick drink. And we just stumbled upon this place and decided to try it.
<v Woman 1>我们在诺斯特兰大道上走，我们只是想找个地方快速喝一杯。我们就碰巧来到这个地方，决定试一试。

00:43:18.590 --> 00:43:19.790
<v Neil Drumming>What are you guys drinking?
<v Neil Drumming>你们在喝什么？

00:43:19.790 --> 00:43:21.000
<v Woman 1>Oh, rum punch.
<v Woman 1>哦，朗姆潘趣酒。

00:43:21.000 --> 00:43:21.810
<v Woman 2>Rum punch.
<v Woman 2>朗姆潘趣酒。

00:43:21.810 --> 00:43:22.010
<v Woman 2>[LAUGHING]
<v Woman 2>[笑]

00:43:22.010 --> 00:43:24.900
<v Neil Drumming>Some folks hadn't heard anything about the drama from last year.
<v Neil Drumming>有些人还没听说过去年发生的戏剧性事件。

00:43:24.900 --> 00:43:29.140
<v Woman 3>You know, I don't even-- I didn't even know that. I think I'm-- yeah, I didn't know that.
<v Woman 3>你知道，我甚至——我甚至不知道那个。我想我是——是的，我不知道。

00:43:29.140 --> 00:43:35.230
<v Neil Drumming>Some people, like Tara here, had heard about the wall and the alleged bullet holes, and definitely felt some kind of way about it.
<v Neil Drumming>有些人，比如这里的塔拉（Tara），听说过那面墙和所谓的弹孔，确实对此有某种感觉。

00:43:35.230 --> 00:43:43.900
<v Tara>And to put it on display and not be a person of color, and not have been a person who experienced that day in, day out, that's not your story to tell. That's our story to tell.
<v Tara>并把它展示出来，而却不是有色人种，也没有经历过那一天，那不是你的故事。那是我们的故事。

00:43:43.900 --> 00:43:53.100
<v Neil Drumming>But the wall's been plastered over. Work from a local artist hangs there now. From Tara's point of view, the slate has been adequately wiped clean. She's there all the time now.
<v Neil Drumming>但墙被抹平了。当地艺术家的作品现在挂在那里。从塔拉的观点来看，这页已经相当充分地翻过去了。她现在一直都在那里。

00:43:53.100 --> 00:44:01.760
<v Tara>Yeah, I feel like things deliberately changed here. I think there's definitely a different vibe here. I think there's a different culture provided here than that was provided beforehand.
<v Tara>是的，我觉得这里的事情确实改变了。我觉得这里肯定有一种不同的氛围。我觉得这里提供的文化与之前提供的不同。

00:44:01.760 --> 00:44:05.350
<v Neil Drumming>This is Neil-- another black Neil in Brooklyn. Who knew?
<v Neil Drumming>这是尼尔——布鲁克林另一个黑人尼尔。谁知道呢？

00:44:05.350 --> 00:44:14.520
<v Neil>Like, I'll post on Snapchat and I'll have the Summerhill geotag. And somebody will be like, oh, you go to that racist bar? That has happened before on my Snapchat.
<v Neil>比如，我会发布在 Snapchat 上，我会加上 Summerhill 的地理标记。有人会说，哦，你去那个种族主义酒吧了？在我的 Snapchat 上发生过这种情况。

00:44:14.520 --> 00:44:20.180
<v Neil Drumming>He's here nevertheless, undaunted-- in fact, twice in two nights. And the reason he keeps coming back?
<v Neil Drumming>尽管如此，他还是来了，毫不畏惧——实际上两晚来了两次。而他不断回来的原因？

00:44:20.180 --> 00:44:29.810
<v Neil>Oyo, who is now part owner-- I knew him from working at another bar. So I knew that he was working here, or had become an owner here. So I decided to come here to support him.
<v Neil>奥约（Oyo），现在是合伙所有人——我从在另一家酒吧工作时就认识他。所以我知道他在为这里工作，或者已经成为这里的所有人。所以我决定来这里支持他。

00:44:29.810 --> 00:44:44.530
<v Neil Drumming>And like that, I had my answer. I heard this from people inside and out of Summerhill-- staff and bartenders at other bars, regulars and haters. If you want to know why so many people come to Summerhill, look for Oyo.
<v Neil Drumming>就这样，我得到了答案。我从 Summerhill 的内内外外都听到了这一点——员工和酒吧招待，常客和喷子。如果你想知道为什么这么多人来 Summerhill，找奥约。

00:44:44.530 --> 00:45:13.900
<v Neil Drumming>Wallahi Oyo-- he mostly goes by just Oyo-- is the executive chef at Summerhill. He became partial owner of the restaurant shortly after all the controversy began. He comes up with the menu. He picks the DJs. On a busy Saturday night, you might find him standing out front, scanning Nostrand Avenue from beneath a black baseball cap, presumably looking for more customers. The word was, the embattled Becca Brennan took a diminished role in her own business so that a 27-year-old black man could become the face of Summerhill.
<v Neil Drumming>瓦拉奥约（Wallahi Oyo）——他通常只叫奥约——是 Summerhill 的行政主厨。在争议开始后不久，他成为了餐馆的部分所有人。他负责菜单。他选择 DJ。在一个忙碌的周六晚上，你可能会发现他站在前面，在黑色棒球帽下扫描诺斯特兰大道，推测是在寻找更多顾客。消息是，陷入困境的贝卡·布伦南在自己的业务中担任了削减的角色，以便一名27岁的黑人男子能够成为 Summerhill 的门面。

00:45:13.900 --> 00:45:24.060
<v Neil>It's not like she has no involvement. There's a lot of stuff that she still does. But I feel like, mostly, when people come here, they see Oyo.
<v Neil>并不是说她没有参与。她仍然做了很多事情。但我觉得，大多数时候，人们来这里时，他们看到的是奥约。

00:45:24.060 --> 00:45:45.870
<v Neil Drumming>Oyo's wasn't just a symbolic contribution. I spoke to him in the kitchen that he oversees. If you ask him what exactly he and Becca did to get black people to come Summerhill after the protests and the bad press, he'll tell you nothing. He and his partner just weathered the storm, and eventually, people came. But that's not exactly true. Faced with the storm, Oyo sent up flares.
<v Neil Drumming>奥约的不仅仅是象征性的贡献。我和他谈到了他监督的厨房。如果你问他和贝卡到底做了什么，在抗议和负面新闻之后，让黑人来到 Summerhill，他会告诉你什么都没做。他和他的合伙人只是挺过了风暴，最终，人们来了。但这并不完全正确。面对风暴，奥约发出了信号。

00:45:45.870 --> 00:45:56.170
<v Wallahi Oyo>And I said-- I told my friends, yo, come on. I need help. My bar has been protested. I need help. I mean, I need-- you know what I'm saying? I need y'all to come support, because I'm drowning.
<v Wallahi Oyo>我说——我告诉我的朋友，哟，过来。我需要帮助。我的酒吧被抗议了。我需要帮助。我的意思是，我需要——你知道我在说什么吗？我需要你们来支持，因为我快淹死了。

00:45:56.170 --> 00:46:03.390
<v Neil Drumming>And from the look of things, many of them did come. Maybe you've heard, we're a forgiving people. But not all of us.
<v Neil Drumming>从情况来看，他们中的许多人确实来了。也许你听说过，我们是一个宽容的民族。但并不是我们所有人。

00:46:03.390 --> 00:46:13.950
<v Justine Stephens>She's using his blackness, in a way, to build this business and make it cool again, because of her mistake.
<v Justine Stephens>她利用他的黑人身份，在某种程度上，来建立这个业务并让它再次变得很酷，因为她的错误。

00:46:13.950 --> 00:46:31.970
<v Neil Drumming>This is Justine Stephens. She and two of her peers organized the first protests against Summerhill after reading about the place on Gothamist last year. She still refuses to step foot inside. But she has noticed Summerhill becoming increasingly popular with black folks in the last few months. She says that's all Oyo remaking the place's image.
<v Neil Drumming>这是贾斯汀·斯蒂芬斯（Justine Stephens）。去年在 Gothamist 上阅读了关于这个地方的消息后，她和她的两个同行组织了对 Summerhill 的首次抗议。她仍然拒绝踏进里面一步。但她注意到 Summerhill 在过去几个月里在黑人群体中越来越受欢迎。她说这一切都是奥约重塑了这个地方的形象。

00:46:31.970 --> 00:46:57.020
<v Justine Stephens>A lot of their online press is kind of fashioned in a way where it's-- it looks hip. It looks like a club scene. Like, if I take out my phone now and take a look at it-- like Wednesday Night Jump Up, for example. I don't think Caucasian Becca Brennan from Toronto would say "jump up."
<v Justine Stephens>他们的很多在线新闻都是以某种方式设计的——它看起来很潮。看起来像个俱乐部场景。比如，如果我现在拿出手机看一眼——比如周三晚上的 Jump Up。我不觉得多伦多的高加索人贝卡·布伦南会说“jump up”。

00:46:57.020 --> 00:47:06.550
<v Neil Drumming>I mean, wouldn't that be something that is more representative-- that kind of marketing, that kind of branding-- more representative of the community?
<v Neil Drumming>我的意思是，那难道不是更具代表性的吗——那种营销，那种品牌塑造——更代表社区？

00:47:06.550 --> 00:47:09.640
<v Justine Stephens>But is it pandering?
<v Justine Stephens>但这是迎合吗？

00:47:09.640 --> 00:47:24.890
<v Neil Drumming>I don't know. Is it? I mean, if-- it's kind of like, if they advertise for something, they advertise something that people in the community want, or cleave to, or are excited by, and those people come, and they support it, is that pander--
<v Neil Drumming>我不知道。是吗？我的意思是，如果——这有点像，如果他们为某事做广告，他们广告的是社区里人们想要的、坚持的或感到兴奋的东西，而那些人来了，他们支持它，那是迎合——

00:47:24.890 --> 00:47:46.460
<v Justine Stephens>No, I think that's totally fair. I think, if it was any other bar that he decided to open up or join forces with, I think that's fair. But again, the profits are not fully going to him. And he's doing her a big-ass favor by making grits and playing Beyonce.
<v Justine Stephens>不，我觉得那是完全公平的。我觉得，如果那是他决定开设或加入的任何其他酒吧，我觉得那是公平的。但再次，利润并没有完全流向他。他在为她帮了一个大忙，做粗粮并播放碧昂丝（Beyonce）的音乐。

00:47:46.460 --> 00:47:54.790
<v Neil Drumming>On Instagram, Justine accused Oyo of helping Becca Brennan get the quote unquote, "black dollar." And this annoyed the hell out of Oyo.
<v Neil Drumming>在 Instagram 上，贾斯汀指责奥约帮助贝卡·布伦南赚取所谓的“黑人美元”。这让奥约非常恼火。

00:47:54.790 --> 00:48:01.580
<v Wallahi Oyo>I'm doing this for my pockets, you know what I'm saying? This is my business. Don't think that I'm doing this for her.
<v Wallahi Oyo>我做这个是为了我的口袋，你知道我在说什么吗？这是我的生意。别以为我在为她做这个。

00:48:01.580 --> 00:48:07.780
<v Wallahi Oyo>I don't think she's using me. If anything, I'm using Becca, you know what I'm saying? Like, trust me. This is for my pockets.
<v Wallahi Oyo>我不觉得她在利用我。如果有的话，我在利用贝卡，你知道我在说什么吗？比如，相信我。这是为了我的口袋。

00:48:07.780 --> 00:48:28.660
<v Neil  Drumming>Oyo thinks about his pockets a lot. An unapologetic capitalist, Oyo learned from his dad, who owned a nail salon nearby. Later, Oyo himself ran a DVD shop on the block, and then he worked as a chef at a black-owned bar down the street. He said that he loves doing business in Crown Heights, and that people in the neighborhood have been waiting for him to open up his own spot.
<v Neil  Drumming>奥约想了很多关于他口袋的事。作为一个毫无歉意的资本主义者，奥约从他爸爸那里学到了，他爸爸在附近拥有一家美甲店。后来，奥约自己在街区经营一家 DVD 商店，然后他在街对面的一个黑人拥有的酒吧当厨师。他说他喜欢在皇冠高地做生意，社区里的人一直在等他开设自己的店。

00:48:28.660 --> 00:48:41.440
<v Neil  Drumming>Before the protests happened, Oyo convinced Becca Brennan to let him launch a pop-up sandwich shop inside her fledgling restaurant. They were both satisfied with how that went, so they started negotiating for Oyo to accept an ownership stake in the restaurant.
<v Neil  Drumming>抗议发生之前，奥约说服了贝卡·布伦南让他启动一个餐馆内的快闪三明治店。他们对结果都很满意，所以他们开始协商奥约接受餐馆的所有权股份。

00:48:41.440 --> 00:48:53.030
<v Neil  Drumming>Then came Justine and the protesters. Oyo took the deal anyway. It was the open door that he'd been waiting for, and there was no way he wasn't going to walk through it, no matter how many people stood outside, jeering. He became partners with Becca.
<v Neil  Drumming>然后是贾斯汀和抗议者。奥约还是接下了这个交易。那是他一直在等待的一扇敞开的门，无论外面有多少人在嘲笑，他都不可能不走进去。他成了贝卡的合伙人。

00:48:53.030 --> 00:48:54.900
<v Neil Drumming>Were you worried?
<v Neil Drumming>你担心吗？

00:48:54.900 --> 00:49:07.880
<v Wallahi   Oyo>No. My confidence in myself was, no, I'm not worried. Me, you know, I feel like I'm Oyo. I can make it happen.
<v Wallahi   Oyo>不。我对自己的信心是，不，我不担心。我，你知道，我觉得我是奥约。我可以让它发生。

00:49:07.880 --> 00:49:34.660
<v Neil  Drumming>Even Oyo will admit that Becca Brennan made a mistake. And for most people, that was the story-- a white woman who offended some people in a historically black neighborhood. But it's this other, smaller interaction that gets to me more-- two black people, Justine and Oyo, pointing at each other, asking who really belongs here.
<v Neil  Drumming>即使是奥约也会承认贝卡·布伦南犯了一个错误。对于大多数人来说，那是故事——一个在历史悠久的黑人社区冒犯了一些人的白人女性。但正是这个其他、较小的互动更让我触动——两个黑人，贾斯汀和奥约，互相指着对方，询问谁真正属于这里。

00:49:34.660 --> 00:49:55.080
<v Neil  Drumming>When Justine saw Oyo defending his mostly silent white partner, she saw a black man who was willing to sell out his community for bread crumbs. Justine was new to the area, but her father grew up in Brooklyn. She thought that the people in the neighborhood deserved better and that she needed to alert them. Oyo was, in her mind, a puppet.
<v Neil  Drumming>当贾斯汀看到奥约为他大多沉默的白人合伙人辩护时，她看到了一个黑人男子，他愿意为了面包屑出卖他的社区。贾斯汀对这个地区来说是个新人，但她的父亲在布鲁克林长大。她认为社区里的人值得更好的，她需要提醒他们。奥约，在她的心目中，是一个傀儡。

00:49:55.080 --> 00:50:14.250
<v Neil  Drumming>But when Oyo looked out and saw Justine leading a crowd of mostly white protesters, he didn't see a representative of the community. He was from the community. He had a tattoo on his arm of the street sign at Bedford and St. Marks, right up the block. Justine, who was from Norwalk, Connecticut, had chemically straightened hair, and she was threatening his pockets.
<v Neil  Drumming>但当奥约向外看并看到贾斯汀领导着一大群主要是白人的抗议者时，他并没有看到社区的代表。他来自这个社区。他在手臂上有一个贝德福德和圣马可的街名标志纹身，就在街区上方。来自康涅狄格州诺沃克（Norwalk）的贾斯汀，留着化学拉直的头发，她正在威胁他的口袋。

00:50:14.250 --> 00:50:17.550
<v Justine Stephens>I had braids. I'm not trying to look like no fucking white bitch, I'm telling you. I'm telling you--
<v Justine Stephens>我有辫子。我没想看起来像个他妈的白人婊子，我告诉你。我告诉你——

00:50:17.550 --> 00:50:27.690
<v Neil Drumming>Justine sent me this recording from that day. It's noisy, but if you tune out the strands of Whitney Houston emanating from inside Summerhill, you can hear a screaming match erupt between Justine and Oyo.
<v Neil Drumming>贾斯汀那天给我发了这个录音。它很吵，但如果你屏蔽掉从 Summerhill 内部传出的惠特尼·休斯顿（Whitney Houston）的声音，你可以听到贾斯汀和奥约之间爆发的尖叫对峙。

00:50:27.690 --> 00:50:30.000
<v Justine Stephens>Get the fuck out of there.
<v Justine Stephens>滚出去。

00:50:30.000 --> 00:50:33.340
<v Wallahi Oyo>You grow up black and you straighten your hair?
<v Wallahi Oyo>你长大是黑人，然后你拉直你的头发？

00:50:33.340 --> 00:50:53.700
<v Neil Drumming>Oyo goes low almost immediately, attacking Justine for relaxing her hair texture to look like a white woman's while fighting for a so-called black cause. When Justine says that's what black women do, Oyo replies, "Stupid black women." In the days following this eruption, Justine received even more hateful and disturbing messages via Facebook.
<v Neil Drumming>奥约几乎立即开始攻击贾斯汀，攻击她为了看起来像个白人女性而放松发质，同时为所谓的黑人事业而战。当贾斯汀说这就是黑人女性所做的时，奥约回答说，“愚蠢的黑人女性。”在这个爆发后的几天里，贾斯汀通过 Facebook 收到了更多令人憎恶和不安的信息。

00:50:53.700 --> 00:50:55.320
<v Justine Stephens>Can I say all the words?
<v Justine Stephens>我能把所有词都说出来吗？

00:50:55.320 --> 00:50:55.950
<v Neil Drumming>Sure.
<v Neil Drumming>当然。

00:50:55.950 --> 00:51:09.380
<v Justine Stephens>OK. "Go kill yourself, bitch. You're a fucking black bitch who wants to be white, but out here protesting about Black Lives Matter. You got nothing to do. Claim you're so pro-black, but have mad white people protesting with you, like they really give a fuck about you. You're really--"
<v Justine Stephens>OK。“去自杀吧，婊子。你是一个他妈的想成为白人的黑人婊子，但在这里抗议黑人的命也是命（Black Lives Matter）。你没事可做。声称你非常支持黑人，但让疯狂的白人跟着你抗议，就像他们真的在乎你一样。你真的——”

00:51:09.380 --> 00:51:30.760
<v Neil Drumming>The Facebook messages went on and on. They were anonymous, but because the insults were similar to things he'd said to her face, Justine thinks Oyo was responsible. I asked him about it. He doesn't deny that the comments could have come from some of his associates trying to support him in the worst way imaginable, but he insists that it wasn't him.
<v Neil Drumming>Facebook 信息没完没了。它们是匿名的，但因为侮辱与他当面对她说的相似，贾斯汀认为奥约负有责任。我问他了。他不否认这些评论可能来自他的一些试图以最糟糕的方式支持他的同伙，但他坚持那不是他。

00:51:30.760 --> 00:51:58.870
<v Neil Drumming>Oyo told me that he mocked Justine's hair because he was raised in a home where relaxers and weaves weren't allowed, where keeping your hair natural was a sign of how pro-black you were. But then, it was Oyo's mother who ultimately told them to apologize to Justine, because he shouldn't talk to black women like that. He did try. Justine showed me contrite messages he'd sent to her on Instagram. But she's no longer interested in opening a dialogue with Oyo or anyone involved with Summerhill.
<v Neil Drumming>奥约告诉我，他嘲笑贾斯汀的头发是因为他在一个不允许松弛剂和发织的家庭中长大，在那里，保持你的头发自然是你多么支持黑人的标志。但后来，最终是奥约的母亲让他们向贾斯汀道歉，因为他不应该那样对待黑人女性。他确实试过了。贾斯汀向我展示了他在 Instagram 上发给她的悔悟信息。但她不再有兴趣与奥约或任何与 Summerhill 有关的人进行对话。

00:51:58.870 --> 00:52:03.280
<v Neil Drumming>So what would it take for you to visit that bar?
<v Neil Drumming>所以你需要什么才会访问那家酒吧？

00:52:03.280 --> 00:52:10.480
<v Justine  Stephens>I'm not going to. I'm actually afraid to go in there, because I'm not going to-- again, I don't want to risk my safety.
<v Justine  Stephens>我不会去。实际上我很怕进去，因为我不会——再次，我不想冒我的安全风险。

00:52:10.480 --> 00:52:11.710
<v Neil Drumming>Have you walked past it?
<v Neil Drumming>你走过它吗？

00:52:11.710 --> 00:52:13.300
<v Justine Stephens>I walk past it every day.
<v Justine Stephens>我每天走过它。

00:52:13.300 --> 00:52:37.480
<v Neil Drumming>I have to say, it really bothered me to hear about this seemingly unbridgeable chasm between Justine and Oyo. And not just because Justine shouldn't have to fear for her safety in her own neighborhood, and not just because the debate they're essentially having over who is a bigger sellout and who is more authentically black is an ugly, unwinnable contest and more than a little absurd.
<v Neil Drumming>我不得不说，听到关于贾斯汀和奥约之间这种看似无法弥合的鸿沟，这真让我感到困扰。不仅仅是因为贾斯汀不应该在自己的社区里害怕自己的安全，也不仅仅是因为他们本质上在争论谁是更大的出卖者，谁更真实地是黑人，这是一个丑陋的、无法赢的竞争，而且有点荒谬。

00:52:37.480 --> 00:52:53.840
<v Neil Drumming>But mostly, it upsets me simply because she's 26 and he's 27. They're both young and black, with roots in Brooklyn. She likes soca. His bar blasts soca music well into the night. It just doesn't seem like they should be so far apart.
<v Neil Drumming>但最主要的是，这让我心烦意乱，只是因为她26岁，他27岁。他们都很年轻，是黑人，在布鲁克林扎根。她喜欢索卡（soca）音乐。他的酒吧整晚播放索卡音乐。这看起来似乎他们不应该离得那么远。

00:52:53.840 --> 00:53:19.410
<v Ira             Glass>Neil Drumming is one of the producers of our show.
<v Ira             Glass>尼尔·德拉明是我们节目的制作人之一。

00:53:19.410 --> 00:53:43.040
<v Ira  Glass>Act Three, An Awful Place That You're Lucky to Get To. OK, so this is something that never really happens. We have a couple minutes left in the show. And I'm glad, because it lets me read you this thing that I like.
<v Ira  Glass>第三幕，一个你有幸到达的可怕地方。OK，这是从未真正发生过的事情。我们在节目中还有几分钟。我很高兴，因为它让我给你读这个我喜欢的东西。

00:53:43.040 --> 00:53:55.000
<v Ira  Glass>This is kind of tangentially on theme with If You Build It, They Will Come. It's about old age, which, I guess, is a place that is built for us. And maybe we get there. I don't know. Don't think too hard about that.
<v Ira  Glass>这与“如果你盖好了，他们会来”在主题上有点关联。它是关于衰老，我想，那是一个为我们建立的地方。也许我们到了那里。我不知道。别太费劲想那个。

00:53:55.000 --> 00:54:06.430
<v Ira  Glass>Donald Hall died a few weeks ago. He was on the show years back, and I knew him a tiny bit. He was a poet and writer, the poet laureate of the United States for a while. He wrote about lots of things.
<v Ira  Glass>唐纳德·霍尔（Donald Hall）几周前去世了。多年前他在节目中，我稍微认识他一点。他是一位诗人和作家，曾担任一段时间的美国桂冠诗人。他写过很多事情。

00:54:06.430 --> 00:54:19.100
<v Ira  Glass>But in the last few years, he's been writing about old age in this utterly unsentimental, lucid way that I really, really love, like in this piece. This is from his book, Essays After 80.
<v Ira  Glass>但在过去的几年里，他一直以这种完全非感伤、清醒的方式写作关于衰老，我真的很喜欢，比如在这篇文章中。这来自他的书《80岁后的散文》（Essays After 80）。

00:54:19.100 --> 00:54:33.510
<v Ira  Glass>"After a life of loving the old, by natural law, I turned old myself. Decade follows each other. 30 was terrifying. 40, I never noticed because I was drunk. 50 was best, with a total change of life. 60 extended the bliss of 50.
<v Ira  Glass>“在爱了一辈子老人之后，根据自然规律，我自己也变老了。十年接着十年。30岁是可怕的。40岁，我没注意到，因为我喝醉了。50岁是最好的，生活发生了彻底的改变。60岁延续了50岁的幸福。

00:54:33.510 --> 00:54:50.080
<v Ira  Glass>Then came my cancers, Jane's death." Jane was his wife. "And over the years, I traveled to another universe. However alert we are, however much we think we know what will happen, antiquity remains an unknown, unanticipated galaxy.
<v Ira  Glass>然后是我的癌症，简的去世。”简是他妻子。“这些年来，我旅行到了另一个宇宙。无论我们多么警觉，无论我们认为我们多么知道会发生什么，古老仍然是一个未知的、未预料到的银河系。

00:54:50.080 --> 00:55:03.660
<v Ira  Glass>It's alien. And old people are a separate form of life. They have green skin, with two heads that sprout antennae. They can be pleasant. They can be annoying. In the supermarket, those old ladies won't get out of my way.
<v Ira  Glass>它是陌生的。而老年人是另一种生命形式。他们有绿色的皮肤，长着两颗头，长着触角。他们可以是令人愉快的。他们可以是恼人的。在超市里，那些老太太不会从我路上让开。

00:55:03.660 --> 00:55:20.930
<v Ira  Glass>Most important, they are permanently other. When we turn 80, we understand that we are extraterrestrial. If we forget for a moment that we're old, we're reminded when we try to stand up and when we encounter someone young, who appears to observe green skin, extra heads, and protuberances.
<v Ira  Glass>最重要的是，他们是永久的异类。当我们80岁时，我们明白我们是外星人。如果我们有一会儿忘记了我们老了，当我们试图站起来遇到一个年轻人时，就会被提醒，他似乎观察到了绿色的皮肤、多出的头和突起。

00:55:20.930 --> 00:55:40.070
<v Ira  Glass>People's response to our separateness can be callous, can be good-hearted, and is always condescending. When a woman writes to the newspaper approving of something that I've done, she calls me a nice old gentleman. She intends to praise me with 'nice' and 'gentleman.' 'Old' is true enough. And she lets us know that I am not a grumpy old fart.
<v Ira  Glass>人们对我们独立性的反应可能是冷酷的，可能是善意的，而且总是居高临下的。当一位女士写信给报纸赞许我所做的事情时，她称我为一位好心的老绅士。她打算用“好心”和“绅士”来赞美我。“老”是足够真实的。她让我们知道我不是一个脾气暴躁的老屁。

00:55:40.070 --> 00:55:51.010
<v Ira  Glass>But 'nice' and 'gentleman' put me in a box where she can rub my head and hear me purr. Or maybe she would prefer me to wag my tail, lick her hand, and make ingratiating dog noises."
<v Ira  Glass>但“好心”和“绅士”把我关在一个盒子里，在那里她可以摸摸我的头，听到我打呼噜。或者也许她更愿意让我摇尾巴、舔她的手，并发出讨好的狗叫声。”

00:55:51.010 --> 00:56:19.200
<v Ira  Glass>Anyway, that's by Donald Hall, from his book, Essays After 80. He just published another book on the subject with the vivid name, A Carnival of Losses. I recommend both books, or especially his book, Without, which is poetry that he wrote when his wife, Jane Kenyon, died. Anyway, thanks, Donald.
<v Ira  Glass>总之，那是唐纳德·霍尔，来自他的书《80岁后的散文》。他刚刚出版了另一本关于这个主题的书，有着生动的名字《损失的嘉年华》（A Carnival of Losses）。我推荐这两本书，或者特别是他的书《没有》（Without），那是他在妻子简·凯尼恩（Jane Kenyon）去世时写的诗。总之，谢谢，唐纳德。

00:56:19.200 --> 00:56:37.750
<v Ira Glass>Our program was produced today by Alvin Melathe and Neil Drumming. People who helped make the show today-- Dana Chivvis, Jarrett Floyd, Damien Graef, Chana Joffe-Walt, David Kestenbaum, Seth Lind, Angelina Mosher, Lulu Miller, Nadia Reiman, Robyn Simeon, Christopher Swetala, Matt Tierney, Sharif Youssef, Julie Whitaker, and Diane Wu. Our senior producer is Brian Reed. Our managing editor is Susan Burton.
<v Ira Glass>我们今天的节目由阿尔文·梅拉特（Alvin Melathe）和尼尔·德拉明制作。今天帮助制作节目的人——达娜·奇维斯（Dana Chivvis）、贾瑞特·弗洛伊德（Jarrett Floyd）、达米恩·格拉夫（Damien Graef）、查娜·乔夫-沃尔特（Chana Joffe-Walt）、大卫·凯斯滕鲍姆（David Kestenbaum）、塞斯·林德（Seth Lind）、安吉丽娜·莫舍（Angelina Mosher）、露露·米勒（Lulu Miller）、纳迪亚·雷曼（Nadia Reiman）、罗宾·西蒙（Robyn Simeon）、克里斯托弗·斯韦塔拉（Christopher Swetala）、马特·蒂尔尼（Matt Tierney）、沙里夫·尤素福（Sharif Youssef）、朱莉·惠特克（Julie Whitaker）和黛安·吴（Diane Wu）。我们的高级制作人是布莱恩·里德（Brian Reed）。我们的执行编辑是苏珊·伯顿（Susan Burton）。

00:56:37.750 --> 00:56:58.260
<v Ira Glass>Special thanks today to Emma Whitford, Sai Mokhtari, Rebecca Birmingham, Simone Polanen, Sarah Sarahsen, Alex Blumberg, Peter Leonard, Lisa Pollak, and Haley Shaw, who did a lot of the music in our church story today. Our website, thisamericanlife.org, where you can listen to our archive of over 600 episodes for absolutely free. This American Life is delivered to public radio stations by PRX, the Public Radio Exchange.
<v Ira Glass>特别感谢今天的艾玛·惠特福德（Emma Whitford）、赛·莫赫塔里（Sai Mokhtari）、丽贝卡·伯明翰（Rebecca Birmingham）、西蒙·波拉宁（Simone Polanen）、萨拉·萨拉森（Sarah Sarahsen）、亚历克斯·布隆伯格（Alex Blumberg）、彼得·伦纳德（Peter Leonard）、丽莎·波拉克（Lisa Pollak）和海莉·肖（Haley Shaw），他们今天在我们教会故事中做了很多音乐。我们的网站 thisamericanlife.org，在那里你可以完全免费收听我们超过600集的档案。《This American Life》由 PRX（公共广播交易所）传送到公共广播电台。

00:56:58.260 --> 00:56:58.910
<v Ira Glass>[ACKNOWLEDGEMENTS]
<v Ira Glass>[致谢]

00:56:58.910 --> 00:57:09.040
<v Ira Glass>Thanks as always to our program's cofounder, Mr. Torey Malatia. And now, he is so generous, he agreed to cater and also costar in Shonda Rhimes' new Netflix series about the music business--
<v Ira Glass>一如既往感谢我们节目的联合创始人托瑞·马拉蒂亚（Torey Malatia）先生。现在，他非常慷慨，他同意在珊达·莱梅斯（Shonda Rhimes）关于音乐产业的新 Netflix 系列中提供餐饮并联合主演——

00:57:09.040 --> 00:57:14.410
<v Justine Stephens>Doing her a big-ass favor by making grits and playing Beyonce.
<v Justine Stephens>为她帮了一个大忙，做粗粮并播放碧昂丝的音乐。

00:57:14.410 --> 00:57:23.990
<v Ira Glass>I'm Ira glass, back next week with more stores of This American Life.
<v Ira Glass>我是艾拉·格拉斯，下周带着更多《This American Life》的故事回来。

00:57:23.990 --> 00:57:28.990
<v Ira Glass>[MUSIC - "HOLD ON I'M COMING" BY SAM AND DAVE]
<v Ira Glass>[音乐 - Sam and Dave 的《Hold On I'm Coming》]
